window.DOCS_INDEX=[{"t":"Cat-GDI8 documentation","u":"index.html","x":"How to read the docs How to read diagrams Blocks, icons and symbols in the structure diagrams Icons What each diagram icon means. Abbreviations TDC, MAP, PFI, EGT and other terms explained. Level 1. Introduction The control system and injection layouts. Basic knowledge of engines is assumed. What an ECU is The control loop: sensors, model, actuators Injection types PFI, GDI and hybrid Engine layouts Configurator: what your engine needs Level 2. How it works How the unit thinks and makes decisions. Torque-based control How the lever becomes air and spark Air and fuel Filling, mixture, injection, pressure, cams Rev limiter and propeller Protecting the engine when the propeller unloads Engine start From key-on to idle Protections and reactions What the unit does on a fault One unit, different engines What is described as data Level 3. Build and set up What to do, in order, on your engine. Step-by-step build From preparation to first start and tuning Working with calibrations Maps, axes, saving, files. Tools Calculators and live diagrams Level 4. Modules in detail Every component, as in the application. Described in detail: 70 of 80 sections. Engine Cylinders, displacement, installed c"},{"t":"Notation","u":"basics/index.html","x":"How to read diagrams Blocks, icons and symbols in the structure diagrams Icons What each diagram icon means. Abbreviations TDC, MAP, PFI, EGT and other terms explained."},{"t":"How to read diagrams","u":"basics/diagrams.html","x":"Reading order Read from left to right The signal travels along the arrows from inputs on the left to outputs on the right. Look at the colour A block's colour tells its type: input, table, arithmetic, logic, dynamics or output. Find the key block The decision is often made by a Minimum block: the strictest limit passes. Open the submodel A Submodel block has its own diagram, described on that function's page. An unfamiliar abbreviation Look it up under Abbreviations, and an icon under Icons. Block types Inputs and outputs Input signal Blue oval: a sensor or engine state Output Green oval: the function result Calibration Grey dashed block: a setting you can change Constant Grey block: a fixed number Tables 2D curve Y = f(X), 16 points 3D map Z = f(X, Y), 32×32, corrections 16×16 Arithmetic Sum Y = A ± B ± C, the sign is at the arrow Multiply Y = A × B Divide Y = A ÷ B Square root Y = √A Minimum Y = min(A, B): the smaller passes Maximum Y = max(A, B): the larger passes Range limiter Y = X within Min…Max Logic Switch C = 0: Y = A; C = 1: Y = B Compare Y = A > B AND Y = 1 if all inputs are 1 OR Y = 1 if at least one input is 1 NOT Y = not X Rising edge Y = 1 at the 0 → 1 transition Hys"},{"t":"Icons","u":"basics/icons.html","x":"All icons An icon next to an input or output shows what kind of quantity it is. Crankshaft, rpm Camshaft, phase Temperature Coolant temperature Pressure Oil Air, filling Throttle lever Lambda, mixture Knock Battery voltage Exhaust Fuel Fuel level Time, delay Start, starter Fault Boat, load Torque Idle Limit Protection Engine health Get-home mode Consumption, range Speed governor Control unit Recording, log Communication (Ethernet, CAN) Injector Spark, coil Throttle Pump Valve Cam phaser Relay, power output Propeller Engine stopped Target, setpoint Adaptation, learning Timer, raster Lamp, signal"},{"t":"Abbreviations","u":"basics/abbr.html","x":"Filter Engine ICE Internal combustion engine A piston engine. TDC Top dead centre The highest piston position, the reference for angles. BDC Bottom dead centre The lowest piston position. Crankshaft Crankshaft The shaft the unit uses to track engine angle. SA Spark advance How many degrees before TDC the spark fires. MBT Maximum Brake Torque The spark angle that gives the highest torque. Idle Idle Engine running with no torque request from the lever. Efficiency Efficiency The share of energy turned into useful work. VE Volumetric Efficiency How much air a cylinder takes relative to its volume. rl Relative filling Air mass in the cylinder relative to nominal, in percent. Fuel and mixture GDI Gasoline Direct Injection Fuel is injected directly into the cylinder. PFI Port Fuel Injection Fuel is injected into the intake port before the valve. HPFP High-pressure fuel pump Builds pressure in the GDI rail. AFR Air-Fuel Ratio The mass ratio of air to fuel. λ Lambda Mixture relative to ideal: 1 is ideal, below 1 is rich, above 1 is lean. WBO Wideband oxygen sensor Measures λ over a wide range. E85 85% ethanol fuel A blend of ethanol and gasoline. SOI Start Of Injection The angle at which in"},{"t":"What an ECU is","u":"learn/ecu.html","x":"The control loop The ECU works in a closed loop: it measures the engine state, calculates cylinder filling, fuel mass and spark timing, and commands the actuators. The result is measured again, so the unit compensates for temperature, pressure, wear and part-to-part variation. Sensors crank and cam angle, MAP , temperatures, rail pressure, lever, lambda, knock ↓ Model in the ECU torque, air, fuel mass, injection timing, spark timing ↓ Actuators injectors, coils, throttle, high-pressure pump, wastegate, cam phasers ↓ Engine its response returns through the sensors Time scale In a four-stroke engine each cylinder fires once every two revolutions. That sets the time the unit has to prepare the next injection and spark. Engine speed Cylinders Firings per second - Between firings - What Cat-GDI8 is An ECU for gasoline engines of up to 8 cylinders with direct injection ( GDI ), port injection ( PFI ) or a hybrid layout, with torque-based control, electronic throttle, boost and cam phasers. The whole unit On the left what the unit sees, in the middle what it decides (everything in torque), on the right what it drives. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. D"},{"t":"Injection types","u":"learn/injection.html","x":"Injection layouts Layouts differ in where fuel is delivered, at what pressure and when in the cycle. Port (PFI) Direct (GDI) Hybrid Injector in the intake port Pressure: typically 3–6 bar. Fuel is delivered into the intake port ahead of the valve, usually before the intake stroke. The mixture has time to prepare, but part of the fuel forms a film on the port walls. Pros: simple fuel system, good homogenization, fuel washes the valves. Cons: transport delay from the wall film, less charge cooling. Injector in the cylinder Pressure: typically 50–350 bar. Fuel is delivered straight into the combustion chamber. Pressure comes from a cam-driven high-pressure pump; the injector is opened by a driver with a high-voltage current peak and a hold phase. Pros: precise fuel per cycle, multiple injections, charge cooling and a larger knock margin. Cons: complex driver and pump, carbon build-up on intake valves, limited mixing time at high rpm. Two injectors per cylinder Pressure: each injector has its own. For each cylinder the ECU splits the fuel per cycle between the GDI and PFI injectors depending on the operating point. Pros: extra flow capacity, clean valves, flexibility across operating p"},{"t":"Engine layouts","u":"learn/schemes.html","x":"Engine configurator Choose a configuration to see the required components and the application modules where they are set up. Injection Direct (GDI) Port (PFI) Hybrid Air Naturally aspirated One turbo Two turbos Throttles One Two Cam phasers None Intake only Intake and exhaust Electronic throttle and torque request There is no mechanical link between the lever and the throttle. Lever position sets a torque request ; the coordinator takes idle control and limits into account, then the model calculates the required filling and throttle position. This is why the rev limiter and protections act regardless of lever position. Load source: MAP or throttle MAP sensor Throttle Speed-density. Filling is calculated from intake pressure, rpm, temperature and cam positions. The main mode; it also works with boost. Alpha-N. Load is taken from throttle angle and rpm. Used when MAP fails and on engines with unstable vacuum, for example with individual throttle bodies."},{"t":"Torque-based control","u":"how/torque.html","x":"Torque-based control In progress The unit does not convert the throttle lever directly into throttle opening. The lever is a torque request . The model decides how to deliver this torque — with air and spark timing — and all limits act on torque. This is the same principle as in modern OEM engine management, adapted to an airboat: engine, gearbox and a heavy propeller. Lever torque request from lever position and rpm Idle controller keeps idle speed, holds a torque reserve ↓ Coordination highest request wins, then every limit is applied Limits rev limiter, protections, start, limp modes — limits always win ↓ Torque target request + friction and pumping losses ↓ Air path · slow cylinder filling → intake pressure → throttle angle, cam targets, boost Spark path · fast spark retard from optimum for quick torque changes Emergency cut separate setting, off by default Model levels Position and time — crankshaft and four camshafts give the engine angle and cam phases. Sensors — every value has a validity flag; a failed sensor is replaced by a substitute or model value. Engine model — cylinder filling, residual gas, spark and mixture efficiency, losses, exhaust temperature. Torque structure"},{"t":"Air and fuel","u":"how/air-fuel.html","x":"Air and fuel In progress Cylinder filling Air mass is calculated from intake pressure with a linear model: slope and offset depend on rpm and on intake and exhaust cam positions, with a temperature correction. Each bank is calculated separately because each has its own cam phases. The model is easy to invert: from the required air mass the unit gets the required intake pressure and, through the throttle flow model, the throttle angle. Fuel Fuel mass per cylinder = air mass / (stoichiometric ratio × target lambda). Target lambda comes from the base map, start and warm-up corrections, component protection enrichment and the wideband correction. Hybrid injection For each cylinder the calculated fuel is split between the direct injector and the port injector. Direct injection can use 1–3 pulses per cycle with a high-voltage current profile from the calibration; start of injection depends on rpm and load. Injector open time is calculated from the injector model for the actual pressure difference. Rail pressure Two high-pressure pumps, normally one per bank. Target pressure is a 32×32 map of rpm and load; control combines feed-forward from fuel demand with a rail model and integral actio"},{"t":"Rev limiter and propeller","u":"how/limiter.html","x":"Rev limiter and propeller protection In progress An unloaded propeller can over-rev the engine almost instantly, so the limiter works ahead: it takes shaft acceleration into account and removes torque smoothly in a band below the limit, quickly with spark, then with air. Emergency fuel cut on extreme over-rev is a separate setting and is off by default. In progress Torque rises through a soft zone that takes up driveline backlash, then faster. Rise and fall rates are curves over rpm. Planned A drive model (engine, gearbox, propeller), damping of torsional oscillations with spark timing, and a keep-out band around the driveline resonance."},{"t":"Engine start","u":"how/start.html","x":"Engine start In progress The strategy counts events (injection number, engine cycles) rather than seconds. Everything below is implemented in code and tested on the model; it has not yet been verified on a running engine. Phase What happens Status Prime Lift pumps run, the unit waits for cranking. In progress Quick start Crank position is found from the camshaft shape before the gap; the gap then confirms it. In progress Waiting for pressure No injection until the rail reaches the first-injection pressure. In progress High pressure Injection on the compression stroke. In progress Low-pressure fallback If pressure is not reached within a set number of revolutions, injection moves to the intake stroke with a long pulse. In progress Start dose Base dose in mg by temperature, corrected by injection number, pressure and rpm. In progress Flood clear Lever fully open while cranking: no fuel is injected. In progress Repeat and hot start Separate dose corrections. In progress After start Extra fuel fades over engine cycles, raised idle, hand-over to the torque model. In progress Warm-up Lambda correction by coolant temperature. In progress Adaptation Basic learning of the start dose. In pro"},{"t":"Protections and reactions","u":"how/protection.html","x":"Protections and fault reactions In progress The main requirement: the boat must get back to shore . Every fault has a code in OBD format and a reaction level. If several are active, the strictest applies. Torque is reduced smoothly, without cutting fuel or spark. Level Reaction Examples R0 Fault code only Knock sensor noise, wideband sensor R1 Torque and filling limit MAP sensor replaced by model R2 Limp mode: as R1 plus an rpm limit (~2500) to get home Throttle not responding, both lever channels or rail sensor failed R3 Engine stop Not assigned to any fault by default Coolant, oil, exhaust and gearbox temperatures and oil pressure reduce torque in four steps. The lowest step is a “get home” mode, not zero. Injection and spark are timed by hardware timers. A silent second core is detected as a fault. An external power-management chip with a watchdog supervises the processor. Stepped protections Each cause gives only a torque fraction from 0 to 1, and the strictest applies. The lowest step is a get-home mode, not zero. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic."},{"t":"One unit, different engines","u":"how/principle.html","x":"One unit, different engines Ready Cat-GDI8 is not built for one specific engine. Everything that depends on the engine and its wiring is described as data in BrainBus GDI-8 and stored in the unit. The firmware is the same for every installation. The control model works with logical values — intake pressure, intake cam position of bank A, the phaser valve of bank A. The configuration decides where each value comes from and which physical pin each output uses. The application shows which pins are suitable for each function and checks the result: one pin — one function. Up to 8 cylinders, firing order and cylinder angles (even and uneven V8, V6…), bank of each cylinder. Injectors: flow at reference pressure, opening delay from voltage and pressure, minimum pulse. Coils: passive 12 V or with built-in igniter 5 V; dwell from battery voltage. 0–4 camshafts with roles (intake/exhaust, bank A/B), each with its own wheel and phaser. Boost: none, one or two turbos; wastegate pneumatic or electric; 1 or 2 throttles. Knock sensors 0–2, wideband lambda 0–2, propeller drive parameters if known."},{"t":"Step-by-step build","u":"build/index.html","x":"Important: the firmware is in development and this guide has not yet been verified on a running engine. Do not skip stages. 1. Choose your engine The guide shows only the steps you need. Your choice is remembered in the browser. Injection Direct (GDI) Port (PFI) Hybrid Air Naturally aspirated One turbo Two turbos Throttles One Two Cam phasers None Intake only Intake and exhaust 2. Follow the stages 1. Preparation and safety A first start always carries risk: backfire, fuel leak, starter shock. 2. Connect the ECU Make sure the unit is alive and sees itself before touching any actuator. 3. Describe the engine The unit is universal, so first tell it what engine you have. 4. Check the sensors With wrong sensors the unit calculates the wrong dose and the wrong angle. 5. Check the actuators Each output must fire where intended. Engine stopped, no fuel. 6. Set up the trigger The foundation of everything. Cranking without fuel and without spark. 7. TDC and spark The unit must know where top dead center of cylinder 1 is. Spark on, no fuel. 8. Base maps The first start runs on numbers known to be safe. 9. First start Short attempts, with a log review after each one. 10. Finish the tune The e"},{"t":"1. Preparation and safety","u":"build/01-safety.html","x":"You will need Fire extinguisher Ventilation Charged battery and charger Multimeter Spark plug wrench Steps Remove fire risks Extinguisher at hand, room ventilated, no fuel leaks. Fill the fluids Oil and coolant at the correct level. Check the battery 12.5–14.5 V with no load. While cranking the voltage must not fall below 10 V. Remove the spark plugs Up to and including the trigger stage the engine turns without compression. Put the plugs in the coils and rest them on engine ground. Done when Everything on the list is in place Plugs are resting on engine ground Stop if There is a fuel smell or leak The battery does not hold voltage Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"2. Connect the ECU","u":"build/02-connect.html","x":"You will need Windows PC Ethernet cable BrainBus GDI-8 application Actuators → Power control → Switches Steps Apply power Switch the ignition on. Do not use the starter. Connect to the PC Plug in the Ethernet cable and press “Connect”. Compare versions Check the firmware on the “ ECU ” page. The application and firmware must be the same version. Check power In the power module, the switches are on and there are no faults. Done when The application shows “Connected” Prime mode, 0 rpm No power or injector driver faults Stop if Power or driver faults: do not continue Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"3. Describe the engine","u":"build/03-describe.html","x":"You will need Engine specifications Firing order Harness diagram and pinout Engine → Configuration → Engine Steps Fill in the engine Cylinders, displacement, firing order, banks. Choose GDI injectors Flow at reference pressure and opening delay. Choose PFI injectors Flow and opening delay. Describe boost Turbos and wastegate type. Describe the camshafts Cam roles and phasers. Assign pins Each function gets its own pin, as in your harness. Save to ECU Structural changes are applied with the engine stopped. Done when Only the modules you need appear in the application No pin conflicts Stop if You do not know the firing order or wiring: find out first Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"4. Check the sensors","u":"build/04-sensors.html","x":"You will need Multimeter Sensor datasheets Sensors → Coolant temperature → Calibration & raw Steps Compare readings On a cold engine coolant and air are close to ambient, MAP is close to barometric. Check the rail With no pump running, pressure is near zero. Unplug the connector A fault appears in the sensor errors and the value goes to substitute. Plug it back The fault clears. Verify the calibration Enter the values from the sensor datasheet. Do not guess. Done when Every sensor reads plausibly An unplugged connector shows as a fault Stop if Crankshaft sensor not working Rail sensor not working on a GDI engine Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"5. Check the actuators","u":"build/05-actuators.html","x":"You will need Plugs in coils, resting on ground A helper to watch and listen Actuators → Ignition coils → Outputs Steps Lift pumps Run the test: the pump hums and feed pressure rises. Then switch it off. Coils one by one Which plug sparks? Compare with the cylinder. Never fire a coil without a plug on ground. GDI injectors Rail without pressure, pumps stopped. Click each one and watch the opening current. High-pressure pump valves Listen for the click. PFI injectors Rail without pressure. Click each one. Throttle Check sensors, end stops and travel. Done when Every output fired in its own cylinder Stop if An output does not fire or hits the wrong cylinder: fix the wiring or assignment Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"6. Set up the trigger","u":"build/06-trigger.html","x":"You will need Pump relays or fuses removed Ignition coils supply switched off Charged battery Trigger → Wheel → Wheel Steps Remove fuel Press the fuel-off button on Trigger → Wheel and remove the pump relays. These are two independent barriers. Remove spark Switch off “Ignition coils supply”. Do not switch off the GDI supply for this. Check the signal “Signal” logger, starter for 3–5 s: crank edges are present. Identify the wheel Record 4 s of cranking, then “Write to ECU ”. Check stability “Online”, 5–10 s: sync appears within 1–2 revolutions and is not lost. Set up the camshafts Record the cam shapes and write them to the ECU. Check with plugs in Fit the plugs. Still no fuel and no spark. Sync must not be lost on compression. Done when Wheel identified 720° cycle sync is stable Zero injector pulses in the log Stop if Injector pulses in the log Sync is being lost Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"7. TDC and spark","u":"build/07-tdc.html","x":"You will need Timing light TDC mark on the pulley Trigger → Wheel → TDC Steps Find TDC TDC tab: record while cranking, then “Write to ECU ”. Enable spark Switch on “Ignition coils supply”. The fuel-off button stays on, pumps stay removed. Check with a timing light While cranking the mark must sit at the start angle. Correct it with the “Timing light” buttons. Check every cylinder Timing light on each cylinder's lead: same mark, correct firing order. Done when The mark matches the start angle Sparks follow the firing order Stop if Difference over 2°: repeat and find the cause Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"8. Base maps","u":"build/08-maps.html","x":"You will need Injector data Displacement and compression ratio Start → Start fuel → Fuel Steps Start angle A small advance, around 5–10° before TDC . Never after TDC, or the engine may run backwards. Running angles Conservative, without large advance. Start fuel Dose by coolant temperature. Start pressure First-injection pressure and the low-pressure fallback. Save to ECU Otherwise the settings are lost at power-off. Done when Start angle is before TDC Settings are saved Stop if If in doubt about the angle: do not start Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"9. First start","u":"build/09-firststart.html","x":"You will need Fire extinguisher A helper Log recording Start → Now → Start state Steps Put everything back Plugs, coils, injectors, pump relays. Release the fuel-off button. Start recording Rpm, sync, pressure, battery, coolant. Starter up to 5 s Watch: pressure builds, injections run, rpm rises. Did not start Stop and review the log. If the plugs are flooded: flood clear, lever fully open while cranking. Started Do not rev. Check oil pressure, leaks and temperatures at once. Shut down after 30–60 s and review the log. Done when Engine holds rpm Oil pressure present No leaks Stop if No oil pressure: shut down immediately Fuel leak Backfire Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"10. Finish the tune","u":"build/10-tune.html","x":"You will need Wideband lambda Log recording Fuel & mixture → Lambda target → Maps Steps Idle Speed and torque reserve on a warm engine. Mixture Compare lambda with the target and correct the filling. Ignition Increase angles gradually while watching for knock. Boost Raise in steps, only after the mixture is set. Cam timing Enable after checking oil pressure. Load Smoothly, from light to heavy. Done when Lambda close to target No knock Temperatures normal Stop if Knock Lean mixture under load Overheating Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"Working with calibrations","u":"setup/calibration.html","x":"Working with calibrations Ready Maps are 32×32, curves have 16 points. Each value is checked by the unit; a value outside limits is refused. Table axes are shared: change the rpm axis once and every table with this axis is recalculated, keeping the same values at the same rpm. Pressure axes follow the calibration of their sensors. Structural changes — wheels, output assignment, number of cylinders — are applied only with the engine stopped. Calibration values can be changed while the engine runs. A calibration can be saved to and loaded from a file on the PC. Questions about the documentation: info@ailuohai-tech.com ."},{"t":"Engine","u":"modules/engine.html","x":"Module sections Configuration Tabs: Engine, Model, Errors & limits Description coming ECU equipment Tabs: Channels Description coming Table axes Tabs: Axes Description coming"},{"t":"Configuration","u":"modules/engine/config.html","x":"What is in the application Engine → Configuration Engine Live view in the application. Model 3 Engine displacement Segment lead Stoichiometric AFR Errors & limits Planned Configuration check: channels, firing order, sensors."},{"t":"ECU equipment","u":"modules/engine/caps.html","x":"What is in the application Engine → ECU equipment Channels Live view in the application."},{"t":"Table axes","u":"modules/engine/axes.html","x":"What is in the application Engine → Table axes Axes Live view in the application."},{"t":"Trigger","u":"modules/trigger.html","x":"Module sections Inputs Tabs: Sensors, Errors Described Wheel Tabs: Wheel, Crankshaft, Camshafts, TDC, Adaptation Described Logger Tabs: Signal Described"},{"t":"Inputs","u":"modules/trigger/inputs.html","x":"Purpose This is where you state which ECU inputs the crankshaft and camshaft sensors use and how the signal behaves while a tooth passes the sensor. Everything the unit computes by angle depends on it: injection, spark, cycle phase and cam phasers. How it works The crankshaft connects either to the inductive VR input or as a Hall sensor on DIGITAL_1. There can be up to four camshafts: intake A and B, exhaust A and B, each on its own DIGITAL_1…6 input. One input cannot serve two sensors, and a busy input is unavailable in the list. When several healthy camshafts are present, they all vote for the cycle phase, and a tie is decided by the main one: intake A, intake B, exhaust A, exhaust B. Sensor VR or Hall on the crank, Hall on the cams ↓ ECU input VR or DIGITAL_1…6, each for one sensor only ↓ Signal on a tooth high or low level ↓ Configuration check a repeated or unsuitable input is shown at once Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Trigger → Inputs → Sensors Open the Trigger module Choose Inputs Choose the crank input: VR or Hall Set the level on a tooth in this column Assign an input to each camshaft The main camshaft is shown here Press Write to ECU with the engine stopped Check errors after cr"},{"t":"Wheel","u":"modules/trigger/wheel.html","x":"Purpose This section teaches the unit the shape of the crank and cam wheels and ties them to cylinder 1 TDC . Afterwards the unit knows the exact engine angle and which of the two revolutions of the cycle is running. How it works The unit recognises a tooth not by absolute time but by the ratio of neighbouring intervals, which barely depends on rpm. The gap gives a large ratio and becomes the reference point. After sync each interval is compared with the expected one, corrected for shaft acceleration, so the speed wave from compression does not break the count. The crankshaft cannot tell the two revolutions of a cycle apart; the cam shape does that. If a camshaft gives no phase within 4 revolutions, the unit assumes a phase and while cranking switches it every 3 s until the engine catches. Tooth interval time since the previous edge ↓ Ratio to the expected interval, with acceleration ↓ Edge number normal tooth or gap, angle 0…360° ↓ Phase from the camshaft cycle angle 0…720° ↓ TDC offset angle relative to cylinder 1 ratio = t n / t expected ; shorter than 0.4 · t expected is noise Trigger wheel Change the wheel and speed to see how much time the unit has per tooth and which stroke "},{"t":"Logger","u":"modules/trigger/log.html","x":"Purpose The logger shows every edge of every input: what reached the unit and what remained after the filters. It reveals where sync losses come from: the sensor, the wiring or the filter settings. How it works Each input has two tracks. The upper one shows all edges, with rejected ones marked by colour and a reason letter: D for dead time, S for the window after a spark, L for the level check. The lower one shows what the decoder received. The recording streams directly to the application and is not stored in the unit. Input filter short spikes, default 1.07 µs ↓ Dead time an edge that is too early, default 25% ↓ Spark blanking window after a spark, off by default ↓ Level check DIGITAL_4…6 inputs, on by default ↓ Decoder receives accepted edges only edge rejected if t < D · min(t 1 , t 2 ); angle shift Δθ = 6 · n · Δt Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Trigger → Logger → Signal Choose Logger Autostart: recording begins on cranking Live: stream on a running engine Crankshaft filters Noise protection level Camshaft filters Open the logger Trigger module, Logger section. Record the signal “Autostart” and crank, or “Live” on a running engine. Read the tracks Only a few rejected edges, with teeth arriv"},{"t":"Sensors","u":"modules/sensors.html","x":"Module sections Coolant temperature Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Described Intake air temperature Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Described Oil temperature Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Described Gearbox temperature Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Described Fuel temperature Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Described Fuel level Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Described Manifold pressure / boost Tabs: Calibration & raw, Crank-synchronous, Errors & limits Described Barometer Tabs: Readings, Errors & limits Described Oil pressure Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Described Fuel pressure, low Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Described Fuel rail pressure Tabs: Rails, Sensor A, Sensor B, Crank-synchronous, Errors A, Errors B Described Lever / pedal Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Described Lambda Tabs: Readings, Details, Sensors & heating, Module CAN, Errors & limits Described Exhaust temperature (EGT) Tabs: Readings, Details, Module CAN, Errors & limits Described Knock sensors Tabs: Sensors & signal, Errors & limits Described Voltages Tabs: Readings, Errors & limits Described"},{"t":"Coolant temperature","u":"modules/sensors/clt.html","x":"Purpose The coolant temperature sensor ( CLT ) reports the thermal state of the engine. It is installed in the cooling jacket, usually near the thermostat. The unit uses this temperature to choose the start dose, warm-up enrichment and idle speed, and limits torque in steps when the engine overheats. Important: the sensor is passive. The unit calculates temperature from its resistance, so the calibration must match the specific sensor. How it works The sensing element is an NTC thermistor: resistance falls as temperature rises. The sensor is connected to ground and forms a voltage divider with a 10 kΩ reference resistor from 5 V. Temperature the sensing element heats up ↓ Resistance falls along the NTC curve ↓ Input voltage divider with the reference resistor ↓ The unit calculates degrees using the sensor calibration Sensor characteristic Temperature Resistance at 25 °C β coefficient what is it? Sensor resistance - Input voltage - State - Example for a typical sensor. Take exact values from your sensor's datasheet. What R25 and β mean A thermistor curve is described by two numbers. R25 is the resistance at 25 °C, where the curve is anchored. β shows how quickly resistance falls as "},{"t":"Intake air temperature","u":"modules/sensors/iat.html","x":"Purpose Intake air temperature gives the air density, which determines how much air reaches the cylinder and how much fuel to deliver. How it works The sensor is an NTC thermistor: its resistance falls as it heats up. The unit measures its resistance through a 10 kΩ reference resistor from 5 V and converts it to degrees with a 16-point table. The value is smoothed by a 0.5 s filter. If the signal stays outside its limits for more than 0.1 s, the unit treats the sensor as faulty and uses the substitute value. By default the sensor is on input IO-6. Sensor resistance falls as it heats up ↓ Measurement divider with a 10 kΩ reference resistor ↓ Table voltage → temperature, 16 points ↓ Check plausible −45…170 °C ↓ Value for the engine measured or substitute 25 °C healthy at 50 Ω ≤ R ≤ 92 kΩ Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Sensors → Intake air temperature → Calibration & raw Open the Sensors module Choose Intake air temperature Check the reading Choose the board input Open and short circuit limits Current sensor voltage Go to errors and limits Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Intake air temperature → Errors & limits Red codes are active now The lower bound the unit trusts The upper bound The va"},{"t":"Oil temperature","u":"modules/sensors/oilT.html","x":"Purpose Oil temperature feeds the stepped engine protection and the cam phaser settings, because oil viscosity changes their response. How it works The sensor is an NTC thermistor: its resistance falls as it heats up. The unit measures its resistance through a 10 kΩ reference resistor from 5 V and converts it to degrees with a 16-point table. The value is smoothed by a 0.5 s filter. If the signal stays outside its limits for more than 0.1 s, the unit treats the sensor as faulty and uses the substitute value. No input is assigned by default. Sensor resistance falls as it heats up ↓ Measurement divider with a 10 kΩ reference resistor ↓ Table voltage → temperature, 16 points ↓ Check plausible −45…170 °C ↓ Value for the engine measured or substitute 90 °C healthy at 50 Ω ≤ R ≤ 92 kΩ Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Sensors → Oil temperature → Calibration & raw Open the Sensors module Choose Oil temperature Check the reading Choose the board input Open and short circuit limits Current sensor voltage Go to errors and limits Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Oil temperature → Errors & limits Red codes are active now The lower bound the unit trusts The upper bound The value used on a fault Open the"},{"t":"Gearbox temperature","u":"modules/sensors/gearT.html","x":"Purpose Gearbox oil temperature feeds the stepped protection: on overheating the unit smoothly reduces torque. How it works The sensor is an NTC thermistor: its resistance falls as it heats up. The unit measures its resistance through a 10 kΩ reference resistor from 5 V and converts it to degrees with a 16-point table. The value is smoothed by a 0.5 s filter. If the signal stays outside its limits for more than 0.1 s, the unit treats the sensor as faulty and uses the substitute value. No input is assigned by default. Sensor resistance falls as it heats up ↓ Measurement divider with a 10 kΩ reference resistor ↓ Table voltage → temperature, 16 points ↓ Check plausible −45…170 °C ↓ Value for the engine measured or substitute 90 °C healthy at 50 Ω ≤ R ≤ 92 kΩ Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Sensors → Gearbox temperature → Calibration & raw Open the Sensors module Choose Gearbox temperature Check the reading Choose the board input Open and short circuit limits Current sensor voltage Go to errors and limits Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Gearbox temperature → Errors & limits Red codes are active now The lower bound the unit trusts The upper bound The value used on a fault Open the section Sen"},{"t":"Fuel temperature","u":"modules/sensors/fuelT.html","x":"Purpose The sensor shows the fuel temperature in the system and helps you notice overheated fuel. How it works The sensor is an NTC thermistor: its resistance falls as it heats up. The unit measures its resistance through a 10 kΩ reference resistor from 5 V and converts it to degrees with a 16-point table. The value is smoothed by a 0.5 s filter. If the signal stays outside its limits for more than 0.1 s, the unit treats the sensor as faulty and uses the substitute value. No input is assigned by default. Sensor resistance falls as it heats up ↓ Measurement divider with a 10 kΩ reference resistor ↓ Table voltage → temperature, 16 points ↓ Check plausible −45…170 °C ↓ Value for the engine measured or substitute 30 °C healthy at 50 Ω ≤ R ≤ 92 kΩ Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Sensors → Fuel temperature → Calibration & raw Open the Sensors module Choose Fuel temperature Check the reading Choose the board input Open and short circuit limits Current sensor voltage Go to errors and limits Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Fuel temperature → Errors & limits Red codes are active now The lower bound the unit trusts The upper bound The value used on a fault Open the section Sensors module, Fuel temp"},{"t":"Fuel level","u":"modules/sensors/fuelLvl.html","x":"Purpose The sensor shows how much fuel is in the tank, in percent. How it works The level sensor is a variable resistor in the tank. The unit measures its resistance through a 200 Ω reference resistor and converts the result to percent with a curve. Each tank has its own curve, so it is captured while filling. Float changes the resistance ↓ Measurement divider with a 200 Ω reference resistor ↓ Curve signal → % ↓ Value fuel level in % Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Sensors → Fuel level → Calibration & raw Open the Sensors module Choose Fuel level Check the reading Choose the board input Open and short circuit limits Current sensor voltage Go to errors and limits Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Fuel level → Errors & limits Red codes are active now The lower bound the unit trusts The upper bound The value used on a fault Open the section Sensors module, Fuel level section. Choose the input No input is assigned by default; select it in the Pin field. Record an empty tank Note the reading with the tank nearly empty. Fill in portions After each portion note the reading and percent. Enter the curve Put the points into the table. Save Press Save to ECU. Troubleshooting What you see Usually How "},{"t":"Manifold pressure / boost","u":"modules/sensors/map.html","x":"Purpose MAP is the absolute pressure in the intake manifold. The unit uses it to calculate cylinder filling and to see boost. How it works The sensor outputs a voltage proportional to pressure. The unit converts it along a straight line through two datasheet points. A voltage below 0.2 V or above 4.8 V means an open or short circuit. Intake pressure pulsates, so the unit measures it in sync with the crankshaft, in an angle window, by default on input IO-7. The Crank-synchronous tab sets the window start, width and number of windows per cycle, usually 8 for a V8. Sensor usually 0.5…4.5 V ↓ Two points voltage → pressure ↓ Check 0.2…4.8 V and plausible range ↓ Value for the engine measured or substitute p = p 1 + (U − U 1 ) · (p 2 − p 1 ) / (U 2 − U 1 ) Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Sensors → Manifold pressure / boost → Calibration & raw Open the Sensors module Choose Manifold pressure / boost Check the reading Choose the board input Open and short circuit limits Enter two datasheet points Angle measurement windows Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 Sensors → Manifold pressure / boost → Crank-synchronous Start angle of the first window Window width in degrees Usually 8 for a V8 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Manifol"},{"t":"Barometer","u":"modules/sensors/baro.html","x":"Purpose The barometer measures atmospheric pressure, which is needed for the air calculation and for operation at different altitudes. How it works The sensor is on the ECU board. The unit checks it three ways: against an allowed range, by rate of change, and against MAP on a stopped engine. Real weather does not change pressure faster than 2 kPa in 10 s. If the barometer is faulty, the unit uses MAP on a stopped engine, then the last good value, then 101.3 kPa. Sensor on the board atmospheric pressure ↓ Range 57…108 kPa ↓ Rate no faster than 2 kPa in 10 s ↓ Compare with MAP engine stopped over 2 s, tolerance 5 kPa ↓ Value for the engine measured or substitute |p baro − p MAP | ≤ 5 kPa on a stopped engine Setup 1 2 3 4 5 Sensors → Barometer → Readings Open the Sensors module Choose Barometer The sensor's own reading The value the engine uses Go to errors and limits 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Barometer → Errors & limits Red codes are active now No data from the sensor Jumps or disagreement with MAP Below the range Open the section Sensors module, Barometer section. Check the reading With the engine stopped it must match MAP and the weather report for your altitude. Check the state The For th"},{"t":"Oil pressure","u":"modules/sensors/oilP.html","x":"Purpose Oil pressure is the key lubrication signal. The stepped protection compares it with the lowest allowed value for the current rpm. How it works The sensor outputs a voltage proportional to pressure. The unit converts it along a straight line through two datasheet points. A voltage below 0.2 V or above 4.8 V means an open or short circuit. No input is assigned by default. Sensor usually 0.5…4.5 V ↓ Two points voltage → pressure ↓ Check 0.2…4.8 V and plausible range ↓ Value for the engine measured or substitute p = p 1 + (U − U 1 ) · (p 2 − p 1 ) / (U 2 − U 1 ) Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Sensors → Oil pressure → Calibration & raw Open the Sensors module Choose Oil pressure Check the reading Choose the board input Open and short circuit limits Enter two datasheet points Go to errors and limits Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Oil pressure → Errors & limits Red codes are active now The lower bound the unit trusts The upper bound The value used on a fault Open the section Sensors module, Oil pressure section. Choose the input Select the board input in the Pin field. Enter two points Voltage and pressure from the sensor datasheet. Check with no pressure With the engine stopped, the "},{"t":"Fuel pressure, low","u":"modules/sensors/fuelLowP.html","x":"Purpose The sensor shows the pressure in the low-pressure line ahead of the high-pressure pump. How it works The sensor outputs a voltage proportional to pressure. The unit converts it along a straight line through two datasheet points. A voltage below 0.2 V or above 4.8 V means an open or short circuit. By default the sensor is on the fast input AV1. Sensor usually 0.5…4.5 V ↓ Two points voltage → pressure ↓ Check 0.2…4.8 V and plausible range ↓ Value for the engine measured or substitute p = p 1 + (U − U 1 ) · (p 2 − p 1 ) / (U 2 − U 1 ) Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Sensors → Fuel pressure, low → Calibration & raw Open the Sensors module Choose Fuel pressure, low Check the reading Choose the board input Open and short circuit limits Enter two datasheet points Go to errors and limits Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Fuel pressure, low → Errors & limits Red codes are active now The lower bound the unit trusts The upper bound The value used on a fault Open the section Sensors module, Fuel pressure, low section. Choose the input Select the board input in the Pin field. Enter two points Voltage and pressure from the sensor datasheet. Check with no pressure With the engine stopped, the rea"},{"t":"Fuel rail pressure","u":"modules/sensors/rail.html","x":"Purpose The sensor shows GDI rail pressure. The unit uses it to control the high-pressure pump and to calculate injector opening time. How it works The sensor outputs a voltage proportional to pressure. The unit converts it along a straight line through two datasheet points. A voltage below 0.2 V or above 4.8 V means an open or short circuit. With one rail sensor A is used, with two rails A and B. Pressure is measured in angle windows tied to the pump cams. If windows stop arriving or the voltage is out of range, there is no value and the pump and injection switch to their substitute. Sensor usually 0.5…4.5 V ↓ Two points voltage → pressure ↓ Check 0.2…4.8 V and plausible range ↓ Value for the engine measured or substitute p = p 1 + (U − U 1 ) · (p 2 − p 1 ) / (U 2 − U 1 ) Setup 1 2 3 4 5 Sensors → Fuel rail pressure → Rails Open the Sensors module Choose Fuel rail pressure Current pressure Sensor A setup Second rail sensor 1 2 3 4 5 6 Sensors → Fuel rail pressure → Sensor A Check the reading Choose the board input Open and short circuit limits Enter two datasheet points Angle measurement windows Press Save to ECU 1 2 Sensors → Fuel rail pressure → Crank-synchronous Rail A window s"},{"t":"Lever / pedal","u":"modules/sensors/lever.html","x":"Purpose The lever sensor passes the operator's request to the unit. It is one of the most important signals, so two independent channels are normally used. How it works By default the channels are on inputs AV3 and AV4 and work as mirrors: one rises while the other falls. If they differ by more than the tolerance, code P2138 is set and the lower value is used. If one channel fails, the lever is limited to 50% and rises no faster than 50%/s. If both fail, the lever is 0 and the engine holds 1500 rpm so the boat can reach shore. Two channels A and B, mirrored ↓ Calibration released = 0%, pressed = 100% ↓ Comparison difference above tolerance → P2138 ↓ One channel 50% ceiling, 50%/s rise ↓ No channels lever 0, hold 1500 rpm lever = min(A, B) on disagreement; normal: |A − B| ≤ tolerance Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Sensors → Lever / pedal → Calibration & raw Open the Sensors module Choose Lever / pedal Release and press the lever as prompted The resulting position Open and short circuit limits Go to errors and limits Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 Sensors → Lever / pedal → Errors & limits Red codes are active now Voltage below minimum Voltage above maximum Open the section Sensors module, Lever / peda"},{"t":"Lambda","u":"modules/sensors/lambda.html","x":"Purpose The oxygen sensor shows the mixture in the exhaust, and the unit uses it to correct fuel. How it works Each head can have a narrowband sensor on an ECU input or a wideband through a CAN module. A narrowband only shows rich or lean: by default above 0.6 V is rich and below 0.3 V is lean. For a wideband, the module does the heating and calculation and the unit receives a ready λ . Heating starts gently and full power comes after the dew point, so condensate does not crack the ceramic. Sensor narrowband or wideband ↓ Heating gentle first, full after the dew point ↓ Readiness signal has left the cold zone ↓ Value rich / lean or exact λ ↓ Fuel correction described in the Fuel and mixture module Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 Sensors → Lambda → Sensors & heating Open the Sensors module Choose Lambda Sensor setup Sensor type Narrowband heater output Wideband module number Take EGT from the module Module setup Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Lambda → Module CAN Module setup Read the module settings Write the settings to the module Sensor calibration in clean air 1 2 Sensors → Lambda → Readings First head readings Sensor warm-up stages 1 2 3 Sensors → Lambda → Errors & limits Red codes"},{"t":"Exhaust temperature (EGT)","u":"modules/sensors/egt.html","x":"Purpose The sensor shows the exhaust gas temperature of each head and feeds the exhaust overheat protection. How it works The thermocouple is read by the wideband + EGT module, which sends the result to the unit over CAN . The module compensates for the cold junction itself. The thermocouple type and correction are set in the module. With no sensors, the protection runs on the exhaust temperature model. Thermocouple type K by default ↓ Module cold junction, measurement ↓ CAN temperature is sent to the unit ↓ Exhaust protection hottest sensor or model Setup 1 2 3 4 5 Sensors → Exhaust temperature (EGT) → Readings Open the Sensors module Choose Exhaust temperature (EGT) Exhaust temperature Thermocouple state Module setup 1 2 3 4 5 Sensors → Exhaust temperature (EGT) → Module CAN Module setup Read the module settings Choose the thermocouple type Reading correction Write to the module 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Exhaust temperature (EGT) → Errors & limits Red codes are active now Open circuit or silent module Short to ground Short to supply Open the section Sensors module, Exhaust temperature (EGT) section. Enable the source In Lambda, Sensors & heating tab, tick take EGT from the module. Check "},{"t":"Knock sensors","u":"modules/sensors/knock.html","x":"Purpose Knock sensors are accelerometers on the engine block. Here you state where they are and which cylinders they listen to. Knock detection itself is set up in the Ignition module. How it works The unit converts the signal into g using the sensor sensitivity, for example 26 mV/g. Each cylinder is assigned a sensor. On a running engine the unit compares the background with a corridor over rpm: below it means an open circuit or a loose sensor, above it means a short or a noisy engine. The check runs every 100 ms and triggers only after several checks in a row. Sensor signal in mV ↓ Sensitivity mV/g → acceleration ↓ Cylinder → sensor which sensor listens to which ↓ Background check corridor over rpm ↓ Ignition module detection and retard a [g] = U [mV] / sensitivity [mV/g] Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Sensors → Knock sensors → Sensors & signal Open the Sensors module Choose Knock sensors Sensor signal level Where the sensor is Datasheet sensitivity Which sensor listens to which cylinder Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Knock sensors → Errors & limits Red codes are active now No clock or offset No signal or low background Overload or high background Open the section Sensors module, Knock"},{"t":"Voltages","u":"modules/sensors/volt.html","x":"Purpose This section shows the on-board voltage and the 5 V sensor reference. Measurements, coils and injectors all depend on them. How it works Starter sag is not an error: low-voltage checks begin 5 s after start and require a 1 s hold. Below 9 V the unit does not test sensors and outputs, to avoid false codes. If voltage stays below 12.6 V for more than 10 s while running, idle rises by 200 rpm for charging and returns once voltage is 0.3 V above the threshold. Battery measurement system voltage ↓ 5 V reference sensor supply ↓ Checks normal 10…16 V, 5 s after start ↓ Diagnostics allowed from 9 V ↓ Idle raise below 12.6 V for 10 s: +200 rpm Setup 1 2 3 4 5 Sensors → Voltages → Readings Open the Sensors module Choose Voltages System voltage Sensor reference Go to errors and limits 1 2 3 4 Sensors → Voltages → Errors & limits Red codes are active now Low voltage High voltage 5 V reference out of range Open the section Sensors module, Voltages section. Check the battery Compare with a multimeter at the ECU terminals. Check the reference Reference 5 V must be in the normal range. Check charging With the engine running, voltage must rise above the resting voltage. Review the limits On"},{"t":"Actuators","u":"modules/actuators.html","x":"Rail pressure Target from a map, feed-forward from injector demand, P from a rail model and I near the target. Volume per stroke is converted to valve closing time using the cam profile. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections GDI injectors Tabs: Injector, Curves, Logger, Outputs, Errors & limits Described PFI injectors Tabs: Injector, Curves, Outputs, Errors & limits Described Ignition coils Tabs: Dwell, Current and spark, Outputs, Errors & limits Described HPFP Tabs: Valve, Mechanics, Curves, Controller, Pumps, Test, Errors & limits Described Electronic throttle Tabs: Throttle body 1, Throttle body 2, Spring 1, Spring 2, Stopped engine, Test 1, Test 2, Errors 1, Errors 2 Described Wastegate pneumatic Tabs: Valve A, Valve B Described Wastegate servo Tabs: Servo A, Servo B, Spring A, Spring B, Stopped engine, Test A, Test B, Errors A, Errors B Described Cam phasers Tabs: Intake A, Intake B, Exhaust A, Exhaust B, Intake regulator, Exhaust regulator, Holding (intake), Holding (exhaust), PID (intake), PID (exhaust), Errors Described Power control Tabs: Switche"},{"t":"GDI injectors","u":"modules/actuators/gdi.html","x":"Purpose GDI injectors spray fuel directly into the cylinder at high pressure. This section sets their flow, pulse limits and current shape. How it works A port injector can run from battery voltage, but a GDI injector needs a strong, short current hit. The PT2001 driver first applies the boosted Vboost voltage and quickly raises the current to the opening level, then holds the peak current from the battery and drops to the hold current until the pulse ends. The board has two drivers: bank A runs injectors 1-4, bank B runs injectors 5-8. The logger shows, per injector, how long the current took to reach the opening level and how each pulse ended. Required fuel mass from the mixture calculation ↓ Pulse time flow, pressure difference, opening delay ↓ Opening Vboost, opening current ↓ Peak and hold current from the battery ↓ Logger time to opening current, errors t pulse = m / (ṁ st · √(Δp / Δp ref )) + t delay Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Actuators → GDI injectors → Injector Open the module Choose GDI injectors Driver state Datasheet flow The shortest pulse Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 4 Actuators → GDI injectors → Logger Start recording Time to opening current Pulses without opening current Driver e"},{"t":"PFI injectors","u":"modules/actuators/pfi.html","x":"Purpose PFI injectors sit in the intake and deliver part of the fuel in hybrid injection. They are off by default. How it works The section becomes active when hybrid injection is enabled in the Engine module. The injectors are driven by outputs OUT0…OUT3 of two MC33810 chips. The fuel share and end of injection are set in Fuel & mixture, Injection. If the PFI share needs a pulse shorter than the minimum, that fuel moves to the GDI injectors, because a very short pulse does not open an injector repeatably. PFI share from the Injection section ↓ Pulse time flow, pressure difference, battery delay ↓ Minimum check too short: the share moves to GDI ↓ Output MC33810 g/s = cc/min · 0.0123 Setup Enable hybrid injection Turn on PFI injectors in the Engine module. Open the section Actuators module, PFI injectors section. Enter the datasheet Flow, reference pressure difference (usually 300 kPa), minimum and maximum pulse. Fill in the delay On the Curves tab, enter the opening delay by battery voltage. Check the outputs On the Outputs tab, click each injector with the engine stopped. Save Press Save to ECU . Troubleshooting What you see Usually How to check The section is grey Hybrid injectio"},{"t":"Ignition coils","u":"modules/actuators/coils.html","x":"Purpose A coil stores energy and releases it to the spark plug as a spark. This section sets how long and with what current it charges. How it works The charging time is called dwell. Passive coils are charged by the MC33810 chip: it limits the current to the nominal value, shuts the coils off at the fault current and cuts an excessively long dwell by itself. The unit sees when the current reaches nominal and, with learning on, adjusts dwell so that nominal arrives just before the spark. Coils with a built-in igniter use the dwell from their datasheet. Battery voltage initial time from the curve ↓ Learning nominal 0.3 ms before the spark ↓ Limits min, max, share of the cycle ↓ MC33810 current, fault current, chip limit ↓ Monitoring spark duration t dwell ≤ share · 120,000 / n, ms Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Actuators → Ignition coils → Dwell Open the module Choose Ignition coils Dwell adjustment Nominal before the spark Lower limit Upper limit Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 4 Actuators → Ignition coils → Current and spark Short spark threshold Charging current Above it the coils shut off Limit inside the chip Open the section Actuators module, Ignition coils section. Set the dwell On the Dwell an"},{"t":"HPFP","u":"modules/actuators/hpfp.html","x":"Purpose The high-pressure fuel pump raises rail pressure for the GDI injectors. This section sets the valve, the cam and the pressure controller. How it works The pump plunger is driven by a camshaft lobe. A solenoid valve decides how much of the plunger stroke goes to the rail: the earlier it closes, the more fuel is delivered. The unit calculates the required delivery from the pressure target and fuel flow, converts it to a closing angle and sends a pulse for each lobe, allowing for the valve delay. The controller runs every 10 ms. If the rail sensor fails, the pump is switched off, injection is calculated at lift pressure and power is limited. Pressure target from the engine state ↓ Controller pressure error → delivery, mm³ ↓ Cam delivery → closing angle ↓ Valve pulse with delay compensation ↓ Rail sensor feedback ΔV = V rail · Δp / E Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Actuators → HPFP → Valve Open the module Choose HPFP Valve closing current Keeps the valve closed Longer is an error Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 Actuators → HPFP → Controller Rail and pipe volume How pressure rises The main setting Open the section Actuators module, HPFP section. Set up the valve On the Valve tab, enter the peak curre"},{"t":"Electronic throttle","u":"modules/actuators/etb.html","x":"Purpose The electronic throttle meters the air. This section sets up its sensors, motor and position controller. How it works A motor opens the throttle through an H-bridge and a spring closes it. Without current it rests in the limp position, about 7-8 %. Two position sensors move in opposite directions and the unit constantly compares them. Every millisecond the controller adds the spring holding term, a friction term and a PID on the position error, then scales the result to battery voltage. If the channels disagree for more than 150 ms, the throttle does not follow its target or the bridge fails, the bridge is switched off and the spring returns the throttle to the limp position. Position target from the torque model or start ↓ Feed-forward spring and friction ↓ PID position error ↓ Correction 12 V / U<sub>bat</sub> ↓ H-bridge 2 kHz PWM ↓ Two sensors position and cross-check PWM = (spring + friction + PID) · 12 / U bat Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Actuators → Electronic throttle → Throttle body Open the module Choose Electronic throttle First sensor signal Second sensor signal Sensor inputs The bridge the motor is on Press Save to ECU 1 2 3 4 Actuators → Electronic throttle → Test Where"},{"t":"Wastegate pneumatic","u":"modules/actuators/wgValve.html","x":"Purpose A pneumatic wastegate controls boost through a solenoid valve. This section of the application is still in development. How it works A pipe from the turbo pushes on the actuator diaphragm and opens the wastegate. The valve bleeds off part of that pressure: the diaphragm sees less, the wastegate stays closed longer and boost rises. For now the application lets you choose the wastegate type; the valve output, PWM frequency, duty map and controller come in the next stage. Pressure from the turbo onto the actuator diaphragm ↓ Valve bleeds off part of the pressure ↓ Actuator opens the wastegate ↓ Boost less diaphragm pressure, more boost Setup Choose the type In the Engine module, select a pneumatic wastegate. Wait for valve setup There is nothing to configure here yet: the output, PWM and controller are not added. Troubleshooting What you see Usually How to check The section is grey Another wastegate type is selected Engine module It says In development This stage is not done yet Normal for this version What is in the application Actuators → Wastegate pneumatic Valve A Live view in the application. Valve B Live view in the application."},{"t":"Wastegate servo","u":"modules/actuators/wgServo.html","x":"Purpose An electric wastegate opens the turbo valve with a motor and a position sensor. This type is not selected by default. How it works The actuator uses the same controller as the throttle: bridge, spring feed-forward, friction and PID . The differences: one sensor channel, so there is no channel comparison; no limp position in the middle, the valve is closed without current; and a tracking tolerance of 10 % over 0.5 s. Until the boost controller is connected, the actuator holds a set position: 100 % means the wastegate is open and there is no boost. Position target set value, later from the boost controller ↓ Feed-forward and PID as on the throttle ↓ H-bridge actuator motor ↓ Sensor one channel Setup Choose the type In the Engine module, select an electric wastegate. Open the section Actuators module, Wastegate servo section. Set up the actuator On the Servo tab, choose the sensor input and the bridge. Learn the stops Engine stopped, ignition on. Run auto-tuning The actuator will move, so keep your hands clear. Verify On the Test tab, command several positions. Save Press Save to ECU . Troubleshooting What you see Usually How to check The section is grey Another wastegate type"},{"t":"Cam phasers","u":"modules/actuators/phasers.html","x":"Purpose A cam phaser rotates the camshaft relative to the crankshaft. This section sets up the valves and the cam position controller. How it works The cam is turned by oil pressure directed by a PWM valve. The controller compares the target and current cam position and holds it with a PID around the holding duty. It works only when the engine is running, above 600 rpm, with oil warmer than 20 °C, 2 s after start and at least 100 kPa of oil pressure. Otherwise the valve has no current and the cam rests at its stop. Targets by engine state are not connected yet: in operation the cam holds its home stop, and the application can command a position for testing. Conditions rpm, oil, time since start ↓ Target home stop or test ↓ Cam position from the learned zero ↓ PID and holding by rpm and oil temperature ↓ Valve 250 Hz PWM Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Actuators → Cam phasers → Camshaft Open the module Choose Cam phasers Cam position Valve duty Choose LS1, LS2, LS7 or LS8 10-second chart Press Save to ECU Open the section Actuators module, Cam phasers section. Choose the output Only LS1, LS2, LS7 and LS8 suit the valve: they have protection diodes. Check the valve On a warm running engine, set "},{"t":"Power control","u":"modules/actuators/power.html","x":"Purpose Power switches feed the banks, injector drivers, coils, pumps and starter, watch their current and shut them off on a fault. How it works The board has eight BTS7002 smart switches. Every 10 ms the unit reads the current of each one. A current below the lower limit while on means an open circuit, and an average above the upper limit means an overload: the switch turns off and retries. The switches of one bank share a control pin, so when one trips, the other turns off too. A bank can be switched off by hand only with the engine stopped, and this lasts until the unit restarts. Request bank, coils, pump, starter ↓ Switch BTS7002 ↓ Current measured every 10 ms ↓ Limits open circuit and overload ↓ Fault shut-off and retries I low ≤ I ≤ I high Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Actuators → Power control → Switches Open the module Choose Power control Bank switch current Driver supply Engine stopped only After fixing the cause Coil supply Open the section Actuators module, Power control section. Look at the switches The Switches tab shows each switch's current, state and highest value. Set the limits On the limit tabs, set the working current range. Zero as the lower limit turns off the open-ci"},{"t":"Oil pressure valve","u":"modules/actuators/oilValve.html","x":"Purpose The valve controls the oil pump pressure. This section sets up its output, feed-forward and controller. How it works The valve can be PWM , for smooth pressure, or two-position. PWM starts from a feed-forward curve, and a PI controller on the oil pressure sensor removes the remainder, with battery voltage correction. The logic that picks pressure by engine state is not connected yet, so in operation the valve stays in the safe position with the highest pressure. On an open circuit, a short or if pressure is not held, the valve also goes to the safe position until reset. Pressure request engine logic, safe for now ↓ Feed-forward pressure → PWM ↓ PI controller on the oil sensor ↓ Correction battery voltage ↓ Valve LS output Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Actuators → Oil pressure valve → Valve Open the module Choose Oil pressure valve Pressure now PWM or two-position Valve output Datasheet frequency Press Save to ECU Open the section Actuators module, Oil pressure valve section. Choose the kind PWM or two-position, and what happens without current. Choose the output PWM needs LS1, LS2, LS7 or LS8; two-position can use any free LS1…LS8. Check by hand Set the PWM and watch the pressure. Ma"},{"t":"Pumps & relays","u":"modules/actuators/relays.html","x":"Purpose Two lift pumps feed fuel to the HPFP . This section sets the delivery scheme, operating mode and reaction to failures. How it works There are three schemes. In the first, a mechanical regulator holds pressure and PWM follows an rpm curve. In the second there is no regulator and PWM holds pressure from a sensor. In the third there is both a regulator and a sensor: the target is just below the regulator setting, so the pump delivers only what is needed and the regulator stays as a safeguard. The pumps can run as main and spare or both together. On a failure the unit moves to the other pump, and if there is still no pressure it runs both at 100 %. If the pressure sensor fails, the pumps hold a current. Delivery scheme 1, 2 or 3 ↓ Duty curve and controller ↓ Switches PWM about 50 Hz ↓ Monitoring current and pressure ↓ Failure spare, then both at 100 % Setup 1 2 3 4 5 Actuators → Pumps & relays → Pumps Open the module Choose Pumps & relays Main pump state Spare pump state Reset after repair 1 2 3 4 Actuators → Pumps & relays → Test Pump check Run time Start the check Stop it Open the section Actuators module, Pumps & relays section. Choose the scheme On Pump scheme, set the deli"},{"t":"Start","u":"modules/start.html","x":"Start strategy Counting is by events and the dose is in mg. High-pressure start with low-pressure fallback, flood clear with the lever fully open, then extra fuel fades over cycles. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Engine modes The mode machine from off to running. Losing sync while running is not treated as a stop. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections Now Tabs: Start state Described Conditions Tabs: Conditions Described Pressure Tabs: Rail pressure Described Start fuel Tabs: Fuel Described Start ignition Tabs: Ignition Described Air Tabs: Throttle and idle Described After start Tabs: Enrichment Described Warm-up Tabs: Lambda Described Adaptation Tabs: Start dose adaptation Described"},{"t":"Now","u":"modules/start/now.html","x":"Purpose This screen shows what the unit is doing during the start right now and why. It has no settings and is the main screen for analysing a failed start. How it works After ignition on, the unit waits for the starter and keeps the lift pump running. Once the shaft turns and sync is found, cranking begins. The unit first waits for rail pressure, then injects at high pressure on the compression stroke or at low pressure on the intake stroke. When speed stays above the end-of-start threshold for 200 ms, After start begins, and when the extra fuel has faded, normal running follows. The temperature is stored at the start of cranking and drives all start tables: coolant warms quickly while the chamber walls are still cold. Priming waiting for the starter ↓ Cranking sync, waiting for pressure ↓ Injection high or low pressure ↓ After start enrichment fades by cycles ↓ Running warm-up by lambda Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Start → Now → Start state Open the module Choose Now The start step in words Stored when cranking begins Now and needed Cylinder injection count How many times fuel ignited Open the section Start module, Now section. Read the first line It says in words which step the start is "},{"t":"Conditions","u":"modules/start/cond.html","x":"Purpose Conditions decide when the start is over, when to protect the starter, and what kind of start this is: normal, repeat, hot or flood clear. How it works The start ends when speed stays above the End of start curve for 200 ms. If cranking lasts longer than the starter protection time, injection stops until the shaft has fully stopped. Repeat start: the engine ran for more than 2 s and has been stopped for less than the set time, so the dose is multiplied by its own factor. After a failed attempt within the same time, the injection count continues so that the large first doses do not flood the plugs. Hot start: coolant and air are both at or above their thresholds. Flood clear: the lever is at or above the threshold while cranking, so there is no fuel, spark stays on and the throttle opens fully. The unit knows the stopped time only while it stays powered. Temperature and soak at the start of cranking ↓ Kind of start normal, repeat, hot ↓ Lever flood clear without fuel ↓ Speed above the curve for 200 ms: start over ↓ Timer starter protection Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Start → Conditions → Conditions Open the module Choose Conditions End of start curve Cranking limit Lever threshold"},{"t":"Pressure","u":"modules/start/press.html","x":"Purpose A GDI injector needs pressure. This section decides how long to wait for it before the first injection and what to do if it is missing. How it works Until the rail reaches the first injection pressure, there is no fuel. Once it does, injection happens on the compression stroke. If it is not reached within the set number of revolutions, or later falls below the switch threshold, the start continues at low pressure, injecting on the intake stroke with a long pulse. Below the minimum pressure there is no injection at all. If the start temperature is outside the high-pressure window or the mode is off, the unit does not wait and picks the stroke from the actual pressure. If the rail sensor fails, the high-pressure pump is switched off and the start runs on lift pump pressure on the intake stroke. Rail below minimum no injection ↓ Waiting for pressure or the revolution limit ↓ Pressure reached injection on compression ↓ Not reached or dropped injection on intake, long pulse Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Start → Pressure → Rail pressure Open the module Choose Pressure Curve by temperature Temperature window Revolutions to wait No injection below Pulse limit Press Save to ECU Open the sec"},{"t":"Start fuel","u":"modules/start/fuel.html","x":"Purpose This section sets how much fuel a cylinder gets while cranking. The dose is set in milligrams per cylinder. How it works The base is the Dose curve by start temperature: the colder, the more, because less fuel evaporates on cold walls. Multipliers follow. Decay by injection: a cylinder's first two injections are full, and by the eighth the dose is reduced so the plugs do not flood. By pressure: at low pressure the spray is worse, so more fuel is used. By rpm: no correction by default. Repeat start, hot start and adaptation factors are added. The pulse time is calculated from the actual rail pressure. Dose by temperature mg per cylinder ↓ Decay by injection injection number × temperature ↓ Pressure and rpm multipliers ↓ Kind of start, adaptation multipliers ↓ Pulse from actual pressure m = m T · k decay · k press · k rpm · k repeat · k hot · k adapt Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Start → Start fuel → Fuel Open the module Choose Start fuel Main curve Dose reduction Rail correction Angle at high pressure Press Save to ECU Open the section Start module, Start fuel section. Set the dose On the Dose tab the default runs from 120 mg at −30 °C to 36 mg at 110 °C. First get a start at the curr"},{"t":"Start ignition","u":"modules/start/ign.html","x":"Purpose The map sets the spark angle while cranking by speed and start temperature. How it works At low speed the spark is placed closer to TDC : an early spark on a slow shaft kicks back against the starter. As speed rises the angle grows so the engine catches more firmly. By default the angle runs from 5° at 150 rpm to 12° at 1200 rpm, with another 2° below 0 °C. The spark stays on during flood clear. This map works only while cranking, after that the normal model calculates ignition. Cranking speed X axis ↓ Start temperature Y axis ↓ Map angle BTDC ↓ Coil spark on each cylinder Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Start → Start ignition → Ignition Open the module Choose Start ignition Select cells Enter a value Apply it Press Save to ECU Open the section Start module, Start ignition section. Find the operating point While cranking, the point on the map shows the current speed and temperature. Change in small steps 1-2° at a time, only in the area the start really uses. Smooth it No sharp steps between neighbouring cells. Save Press Save to ECU. Troubleshooting What you see Usually How to check Starter kickback Angle too early at low speed Left part of the map Weak catch Angle too late Cells near t"},{"t":"Air","u":"modules/start/air.html","x":"Purpose This section sets the throttle position with the engine stopped and while cranking, plus the idle speed target by temperature. How it works While the engine is stopped and cranking, the throttle holds one set position: enough air to catch, without a speed flare. During flood clear it opens to 100 % to clear excess fuel. After the start, the idle control takes over the air and aims for the speed from the coolant curve: a cold engine holds a higher speed. Engine stopped set position ↓ Cranking same position ↓ Flood clear throttle 100 % ↓ After catching idle target by coolant Setup 1 2 3 4 5 Start → Air → Throttle and idle Open the module Choose Air Idle target Throttle position Press Save to ECU Open the section Start module, Air section. Set the position The Throttle when stopped and cranking field, 0-25 % allowed. Check the idle speed On the Idle speed by coolant tab. Check during a start Speed must not flare sharply after catching. Save Press Save to ECU. Troubleshooting What you see Usually How to check Speed flare after start Throttle open too far Throttle position Catches and dies Too little air Throttle position, idle target High idle when warm Idle curve too high Righ"},{"t":"After start","u":"modules/start/post.html","x":"Purpose Right after catching, the engine needs extra fuel. This section sets that enrichment and how quickly it fades. How it works The chamber and intake walls are still cold, and part of the fuel settles on them as a film. So a percentage based on start temperature is added to the normal dose: by default from +60 % in deep cold to +8 % when hot. The enrichment fades by engine cycles rather than seconds, so it behaves the same at any speed. By default it is gone after 600 cycles when cold and 160 when hot. When less than 0.5 % remains or the time limit passes, the mode ends. On a repeat start, the enrichment is multiplied by its factor. Catch speed above threshold ↓ Enrichment by start temperature ↓ Decay by engine cycles ↓ End faded or time is up m = m model · (1 + k add · k decay · k repeat ) Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Start → After start → Enrichment Open the module Choose After start Enrichment by temperature How it fades Time limit Press Save to ECU Open the section Start module, After start section. Set the enrichment On the Enrichment tab, set the percentage by temperature: 0.5 means +50 %. Set the decay On Decay by cycles, set what share of the enrichment remains. Check the limit B"},{"t":"Warm-up","u":"modules/start/warm.html","x":"Purpose Until the engine is warm, the mixture is kept richer. This curve sets how much richer by the current coolant temperature. How it works The lambda target from the main map is multiplied by this curve. A value below 1 means richer. In the factory setting the factor rises smoothly from 0.82 at −30 °C to 0.995 at 75 °C, so the enrichment almost disappears by operating temperature. Unlike the start tables, this one uses the current coolant temperature rather than the one stored at the start. The numbers in the screenshot are an example. λ target from the main map ↓ Coolant now curve input ↓ Factor below 1: richer ↓ Warm-up λ target target · factor λ target = λ base (n, rl ) · k warm (T clt ) Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Start → Warm-up → Warm-up enrichment Open the module Choose Warm-up Factor curve Enter a value Apply it Press Save to ECU Open the section Start module, Warm-up section. Find the operating point The green point on the chart shows the current coolant temperature. Set the cold part If the cold engine runs rough, reduce the factor at that point. Check when warm Above operating temperature the factor should be near 1. Save Press Save to ECU. Troubleshooting What you see Usually"},{"t":"Adaptation","u":"modules/start/adapt.html","x":"Purpose Adaptation gradually adjusts the start dose to the real fuel and engine. It is off by default. How it works The unit looks at how many injections per cylinder were needed before catching. A slow start raises the factor by one step. A fast start lowers it by a quarter step. A failed start with fuel delivered for a long time raises it by one step. It learns only from clean starts: no flood clear, not a repeat start, and no continued injection count. There is one factor for all temperatures, stored in the unit. This is a basic version: it does not yet check lambda after the start or exclude starts with a weak battery or low pressure, so turn it on after the dose is set up. Clean start no flood clear or repeat ↓ Injections before catching slow or fast ↓ Factor +step or −¼ step ↓ Limits minimum and maximum ↓ Next start dose × factor Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Start → Adaptation → Start dose adaptation Open the module Choose Adaptation 1 turns it on Factor step Slow start threshold What the unit learned Press Save to ECU Set the dose first Adaptation corrects a tuned dose and does not replace tuning. Open the section Start module, Adaptation section. Check the limits By default the fact"},{"t":"Idle","u":"modules/idle.html","x":"Idle The propeller is always on the shaft, so idle requests torque rather than throttle percent. Functions are enabled in stages: controller, reserve and spark, loads, catch, adaptation. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections Now and stages Tabs: Idle now Described Idle speed Tabs: Target and entry, Idle speed by coolant Described Regulator Tabs: Reserve and ignition, PID Described Loads and catch Tabs: Loads and catch Described Adaptation Tabs: Adaptation Described Faults Tabs: On faults Described"},{"t":"Now and stages","u":"modules/idle/now.html","x":"Purpose This screen shows what idle control is doing now and what its torque is made of, and holds the tuning stage switches. How it works On a boat the propeller is always connected to the engine, so at idle the engine turns the propeller. The unit requests torque rather than a throttle percentage. Two paths deliver it: air through the throttle, slowly, and ignition, quickly, within the reserve. Fuel and spark are never cut at idle. There are four states: lever pressed, lever released with speed still high, holding speed, and catch on a speed drop. Tuning goes in stages, each with its own switch at the bottom of the tab. By default only the speed regulator is on. Target speed coolant curve and additions ↓ Torque request propeller, loads, adaptation, regulator ↓ Air throttle, slow ↓ Ignition fast, within the reserve ↓ Speed feedback T = T prop + T loads + T manual + T adapt + T reg + T catch Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Idle → Now and stages → Idle now Open the module Choose Now and stages What idle is doing Target Why it is above the curve Total requested torque Torque parts Prepare the throttle Run the auto-calibration: the throttle must sit steadily at 1-5 %. Stage 1: manual torque Turn "},{"t":"Idle speed","u":"modules/idle/speed.html","x":"Purpose This section sets which speed to hold and how smoothly to enter and leave idle. How it works The base target is a curve by coolant temperature, 1100 to 750 rpm by default. Added to it are the after-start raise, the low-voltage raise for charging the battery, and the raise when running by camshaft. The target rises at once and falls no faster than the set rate. Idle engages when the lever is below the threshold and speed has entered the window above the target. The lever gap stops the mode from toggling at the boundary. From above, speed approaches the target smoothly, and when the lever is pressed the larger of the two requests is used, so there is no jerk. Coolant curve base target ↓ Additions after start, voltage, camshaft ↓ Limit falls no faster than set ↓ Lever and window idle entry ↓ Smooth approach from above to the target n target = n clt + n start + n batt + n cam Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Idle → Idle speed → Target and entry Open the module Choose Idle speed Target curve After-start raise Lever threshold Entry window Approach smoothness Press Save to ECU Open the section Idle module, Idle speed section. Set the curve On the Idle speed by coolant tab, set the target for"},{"t":"Regulator","u":"modules/idle/pid.html","x":"Purpose Two regulators hold the speed. The main one works through air, the fast one through ignition. How it works The main PID compares speed with the target and changes the torque request, and the throttle carries it out. Air reaches the cylinder with a delay, so fast dips have a second path. The unit holds slightly more air than needed by the torque reserve and sets ignition a little later than the best angle. When speed falls, ignition moves earlier at once and releases the reserve. The main regulator then corrects the air and ignition returns to the middle of the reserve. The reserve never exceeds 60 % of the idle torque. While stage 3 is off, ignition follows the map and only air works. Speed error target − speed ↓ PID torque request, slow ↓ Throttle air ↓ Fast regulator ignition only ↓ Reserve limit of the fast correction ΔT fast = K p · (n target − n) − K d · dn/dt, |ΔT| ≤ reserve Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Idle → Regulator → Reserve and ignition Open the module Choose Regulator PID gains Torque reserve by coolant Fast regulator Response to a fall Press Save to ECU Open the section Idle module, Regulator section. Tune the PID On the PID tab the numbers go in order: kp, ki, kd, D f"},{"t":"Loads and catch","u":"modules/idle/loads.html","x":"Purpose This section adds torque for known loads in advance and saves the engine from stalling on a sharp dip. How it works The unit switches the lift pumps itself, so it knows when they load the shaft and adds their torque at once. The alternator always turns and gets extra torque at low voltage. There is no fan output yet, its value is set for the future. Propeller torque uses a learned coefficient: until it is learned, the regulator does this work. A catch triggers if speed is below the target by the threshold, or will be there after the set time at the current rate of fall. Torque is added at once and then removed smoothly. Catches are counted on Idle now. Load is on the unit knows itself ↓ Feed-forward torque added at once ↓ Speed forecast looking ahead ↓ Catch torque added ↓ Decay removed smoothly n + (dn/dt) · t ahead target − Δn Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Idle → Loads and catch → Loads and catch Open the module Choose Loads and catch Load torque Propeller feed-forward Catch threshold Catch strength Press Save to ECU Open the section Idle module, Loads and catch section. Turn on stage 4 On Idle now. Set the load torque Switch a pump on and watch the regulator part: it must not jump"},{"t":"Adaptation","u":"modules/idle/adapt.html","x":"Purpose Adaptation stores a constant torque correction so the regulator does not have to hold it every time. How it works A loss model is never exact, so at steady idle the regulator constantly holds some torque. Adaptation slowly moves it into a learned correction, separately for a cold and a warm engine, and blends both between the limits. The unit learns only when speed has been near the target for the set time, there is no catch, loads have not switched, there are no faults or limits, and the coolant and intake pressure sensors are healthy. The correction is stored in the unit and erased with a button. Steady idle conditions met ↓ Regulator part constant torque ↓ Transfer share per second ↓ Correction cold and warm ↓ Limit ±20 Nm Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Idle → Adaptation → Adaptation Open the module Choose Adaptation Learned corrections Erase the correction Learning condition Limit Press Save to ECU Tune stages 1-5 first Adaptation is turned on last. Open the section Idle module, Adaptation section. Check the conditions By default 20 and 70 °C, ±40 rpm, 5 s, rate 0.1, limit ±20 Nm. Turn on stage 6 On Idle now. Watch the correction The regulator part at steady idle should go to zero"},{"t":"Faults","u":"modules/idle/faults.html","x":"Purpose The boat must get home. This section sets how idle behaves on faults. How it works If the coolant sensor fails, the target becomes one set number and there is no after-start raise. When running by camshaft, the target and torque reserve are higher, and the fast regulator ignores the speed rate because that signal is noisy. If the lever sensors fail, the target stays at or above the hold speed. At low voltage the target rises for charging. If the throttle is in the fail-safe position, ignition holds the speed even with stage 3 off, and speed may be above the target. Cylinders are not shut off. Limiters and protections do not cut torque below what idle needs, but the rev limiter works as usual. Fault sensor or actuator ↓ Target safe speed ↓ Torque path air or ignition ↓ Adaptation stops ↓ Engine keeps running Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Idle → Faults → On faults Open the module Choose Faults Target on coolant fault Ignition holds the speed Do not cut idle torque Press Save to ECU Open the section Idle module, Faults section. Set the coolant fault target 1000 rpm by default: speed must be stable even on a cold engine. Set camshaft running Target 150 rpm higher, reserve 10 Nm larger. Sav"},{"t":"Boost","u":"modules/boost.html","x":"Module sections Boost target Tabs: Target Described Protection Tabs: Errors & limits Described"},{"t":"Boost target","u":"modules/boost/target.html","x":"Planned. This function is described in advance; part of it is not implemented yet. Purpose The boost regulator is still in development and this section has no settings. This page describes what the unit does with the turbo today and how the regulator will work. The module is available only if boost is selected in the Engine module. How it works Today the unit does not hold a set boost pressure. When the requested torque needs pressure near atmospheric or above, the throttle simply opens fully. An electric wastegate is held 100 % open by default, so the turbo makes almost no boost. A pneumatic wastegate is not regulated by the unit yet, so its spring sets the pressure. Planned: the boost target will come from the torque request with a margin across the throttle and will be limited by speed, air temperature, atmospheric pressure, knock and faults. The wastegate position will combine a known feed-forward part and a PID on the pressure error. The diagram below shows this plan. Torque request required filling ↓ Boost target planned ↓ Target limits planned ↓ Wastegate regulator planned ↓ Boost sensor feedback, planned Setup 1 2 3 4 Engine → Configuration Open the Engine module Choose Con"},{"t":"Protection","u":"modules/boost/protect.html","x":"Planned. This function is described in advance; part of it is not implemented yet. Purpose Overboost protection is still in development and this section has no settings. This page describes which checks already work and which do not exist yet. How it works Electric wastegate monitoring already works: on a sensor fault, tracking error, stiff travel or bridge error, the bridge is switched off, a code is stored and the filling limit drops to 60 %. Without current the valve returns on its spring and may close. The factory filling limit is far above atmospheric, so in that case it will not hold the pressure back. If the intake pressure sensor fails, the unit moves to a calculation from throttle, speed and barometer and remembers that boost may be allowed only after a new start. Nothing uses this flag yet because there is no regulator. Planned: overboost detection with the wastegate fully open and a torque limit, a code for low boost, and boost shut-off on a boost sensor fault. Wastegate monitoring works ↓ Pressure sensor model substitute works ↓ Filling limit works, does not hold pressure ↓ Overboost planned ↓ Boost dump planned Setup 1 2 3 Boost → Protection → Errors & limits Open the "},{"t":"Ignition","u":"modules/ignition.html","x":"Spark and knock Spark is calculated for the required torque. Knock in a cylinder window gives a fast retard with delayed recovery, the average retard feeds adaptation, and an octane factor blends the 92 and 100 maps. Retard causes no torque dip: air smoothly makes up the torque. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections Spark timing Tabs: Maps Described Knock: detection Tabs: Setup, Noise map, Knock threshold Described Knock: retard Tabs: Setup, Retard Described Knock: fuel and fault Tabs: Fuel 92 / 100, Sensor fault Described"},{"t":"Spark timing","u":"modules/ignition/timing.html","x":"Purpose Four maps set the bounds of spark advance. The unit does not read the angle from a single table: it calculates the angle for the required torque and keeps it between the safe and the latest limits. How it works The two working maps are tuned on different fuels. The octane factor k smoothly picks a point between them, and knock retard is subtracted from the result. This gives the earliest allowed angle. When the engine needs full torque, the unit runs at that angle. When it needs less, the unit uses the MBT map and the efficiency model to calculate a later angle, but never later than the Latest map. The engine does not run on the MBT map if it is earlier than the knock limit: the map is used to calculate torque loss. “100” map best fuel ↓ “92” map worst fuel, engine is safe ↓ Octane factor k 0 is the 92 map, 1 is the 100 map ↓ Spark for torque not earlier than the limit, not later than the latest IGN limit = IGN 100 − (1 − k) · (IGN 100 − IGN 92 ) − retard; IGN latest ≤ IGN ≤ IGN limit Spark, 100 map Spark, 92 map Octane factor k Knock retard Spark for this fuel - Final spark - Example calculation. Real angles come from maps over rpm and filling. Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Ignition"},{"t":"Knock: detection","u":"modules/ignition/knock.html","x":"Purpose The unit listens to the knock sensors in a window after each cylinder's TDC and decides whether knock occurred. Each cylinder keeps its own background level, so a noisy cylinder does not trigger false detections. How it works The signal passes through one or two bands at the combustion chamber resonances. The knock feature is compared with the cylinder background: if the window is louder than the background by more than the set ratio, it is knock. The factory threshold is 2.5. There are two sensors, each with its own cylinders. A cylinder whose sensor is not in use has no knock control. The background learns by itself, and if the noise curve is enabled, it starts from that curve and stays in a corridor around it. When filling rises faster than 60 % per second, the threshold is multiplied by 1.3 and the background follows the signal faster. Long knock cannot raise the background to its own level, because background growth per window is limited. Angle window default 10…60° after TDC, from 800 rpm ↓ Frequency bands chamber resonances, second band optional ↓ Feature RMS of band 1 + weight × RMS of band 2 ↓ Comparison feature / background above threshold knock if feature / backg"},{"t":"Knock: retard","u":"modules/ignition/knockRetard.html","x":"Purpose What the unit does after hearing knock: it removes spark only on that cylinder, then carefully restores it. Torque does not dip, because air smoothly makes up the difference. How it works Each knock event adds a step to the cylinder's retard, and stronger knock means a larger step. After a quiet pause the angle returns at a set rate. The average retard slowly moves into a shared adaptation over rpm and filling, and during quiet running the learned value decreases very slowly. Fast retard and adaptation together never exceed the maximum retard. If the average retard is large, the unit also reduces filling: no limit up to 2°, down to 70 % at 10° and above. This map is in the Torque & limits module. Knock in a cylinder retard + step ↓ Limit default 10°, 6° at light load ↓ 0.5 s pause then recovery at 1 °/s ↓ Adaptation 16×16 map, up to 8° step = step(n) · (1 + gain · (ratio − threshold)) Window vs background, times Threshold Retard step Step gain Decision - Retard per event - Firmware defaults: threshold 2.5, step 1.5°, gain 0.5. Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Ignition → Knock: retard Choose Knock: retard Check the retard step over rpm Set the step gain Set the maximum retard Set the recov"},{"t":"Knock: fuel and fault","u":"modules/ignition/knockFuel.html","x":"Purpose The unit estimates fuel quality and picks a point between the 92 and 100 maps. If the knock sensor fails, it moves to the safe map and limits the engine. How it works Factor k falls quickly when the average retard is high and rises slowly only after long quiet running under load. A knock event at that load resets the quiet-running count. After refuelling k returns to the start value, because the new fuel is unknown. The unit notices refuelling both while running and between power-ups. A sensor fault means an open or short on a sensor in use, with the engine running and the supply normal. While the fault is active, the 92 map runs with the safety retard, and the learned k is kept. Start k = 0.5 after reset and refuelling ↓ Knock average retard above 3°: k − 0.1, at most every 5 s ↓ Quiet under load filling from 60%, 120 s: k + 0.02 ↓ Sensor fault k = 0, safety retard 6°, torque to 85 %, filling to 75 % Spark, 100 map Spark, 92 map Octane factor k Knock retard Spark for this fuel - Final spark - Example calculation. Real angles come from maps over rpm and filling. Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Ignition → Knock: fuel and fault Choose Knock: fuel and fault Set the start value Check the ref"},{"t":"Fuel & mixture","u":"modules/fuel.html","x":"Mixture and wideband correction Target lambda comes from a map with warm-up and exhaust protection corrections. Three independent wideband modes run: PI , fast cell correction and long-term adaptation. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Fuel and pulse Fuel per cycle is split between PFI and GDI (up to three pulses). Each part's time comes from the injector model at the actual pressure difference. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections Lambda target Tabs: Maps Described Injection Tabs: Cycle scheme, Start of injection, GDI pulses, PFI Described Fuel pressure Tabs: Target, Rise Described Lambda control Tabs: Modes, Learned, Errors & limits Described Exhaust protection Tabs: Model, Errors & limits Described"},{"t":"Lambda target","u":"modules/fuel/target.html","x":"Purpose This section sets the mixture the unit holds in every mode: the lambda target by speed and filling. How it works The unit calculates the air mass in the cylinder and divides it by the stoichiometric 14.7 and by the lambda target. The result is the fuel mass per cycle in milligrams. The map sets lambda by speed and filling. On a cold engine it is multiplied by the warm-up enrichment curve from the Start module. If the exhaust temperature model is above the limit, lambda comes from the protection map when that is richer. The target changes smoothly, so fuel and torque do not jump. The factory map: lambda 1.0 up to 80 % filling, then richer down to 0.88 at 110 %. Each bank has its own target, because exhaust protection is calculated separately. Air mass Air module ↓ Lambda map speed and filling ↓ Warm-up coolant curve, Start module ↓ Exhaust protection richer when hot ↓ Wideband trims PI , fast, adaptation ↓ Fuel, mg per cylinder cycle m fuel = m air / (14.7 · λ target ) · (1 + PI) · k fast · k adapt Setup 1 2 3 4 5 Fuel & mixture → Lambda target → Lambda target (example values) Open the module Choose Lambda target Lambda map Select the whole map Press Save to ECU Open the map"},{"t":"Injection","u":"modules/fuel/inject.html","x":"Purpose This section sets when and in which parts fuel enters the cylinder: GDI start of injection, splitting into pulses and the PFI injector share. How it works The Cycle scheme tab switches the mode and shows the cycle of cylinder 1 right now. Start of injection is set in degrees before compression TDC . The factory 300° injects on the intake stroke for a homogeneous mixture. With two or three pulses, the second and third take their share of GDI fuel from maps, multiplied by a coolant factor. Together they take no more than 90 %, and the first pulse gets the rest. A part goes back into the first pulse if it is shorter than the injector minimum pulse, if it cannot follow the previous one with a pause for the injector supply to recharge, or if it does not start after the previous one ends. Above the set speed (factory 4500 rpm) and during cranking there is one pulse. With PFI, part of the fuel from the PFI share map goes to the port and the rest to GDI. During cranking only GDI works. After the start the PFI share rises from zero: longer when cold, from 8 s at −20 °C to 1.5 s at 90 °C. If a PFI pulse would be shorter than the minimum, all fuel goes to GDI. Fuel per cycle mg ↓ PFI "},{"t":"Fuel pressure","u":"modules/fuel/pressure.html","x":"Purpose This section sets the GDI rail fuel pressure held by the high-pressure pump. How it works The map sets the target pressure by speed and filling. Factory: 5 MPa at idle, up to 20 MPa at full load. The target changes no faster than the set rate, factory 40 MPa/s. After the start the target rises from the current pressure. For each pump stroke the unit orders a volume: injector use per stroke, leakage, a share of the pressure error and an integral part near the target. The volume is converted into the pump valve closing moment using the cam profile. Above 25 MPa delivery stops and resumes after a 2 MPa drop. If the pressure sensor is faulty, the pump is not regulated. The pump itself and its regulator are set up in the Actuators module, HP pump section. Target map speed and filling ↓ Rise no faster than set ↓ Volume per stroke use, leakage, P and I ↓ Pump valve closing angle by the cam ↓ Rail sensor feedback Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Fuel & mixture → Fuel pressure → Target (example values) Open the module Choose Fuel pressure Target map Rise rate Select the whole map Press Save to ECU Set up the pump In the Actuators module, enter the HP pump data and check the rail pressure sensor. S"},{"t":"Lambda control","u":"modules/fuel/closed.html","x":"Purpose This section sets how the unit corrects fuel from the wideband oxygen sensors so the mixture matches the target. How it works Three modes run, and each can be turned off. The PI regulator compares measured lambda with the target and gives a correction of up to ±20 %. Factory gains: P 0.2, I 1.0. The fast trim starts if the engine has stayed in one cell of the 16×16 grid for more than 30 cycles: that cell's factor takes over the PI correction at a rate of 2 per second, but no further than ±20 %. Its values are kept until power-off. Long-term adaptation very slowly, at 0.05 per second, moves the total correction into its own map for each bank, within 0.75-1.25. The map is stored in the unit. Correction works when the wideband is ready, coolant is at least 40 °C, exhaust protection is off, intake pressure changes slower than 50 kPa/s and 1 s has passed since the last mode change. At other times the last PI correction is kept. If the wideband is lost, the PI correction is reset to zero. Bank wideband measured lambda ↓ Conditions sensor ready, warm, steady ↓ PI fast response, ±20 % ↓ Fast point trim cell, until power-off ↓ Adaptation slow, stored k fuel = (1 + PI) · k fast · k a"},{"t":"Exhaust protection","u":"modules/fuel/exh.html","x":"Purpose This section protects the exhaust manifold, turbo and catalyst from overheating by enriching the mixture. How it works The unit does not use an exhaust gas temperature sensor, and the temperature comes from a model. The map sets the steady temperature by speed and filling. Factory: from 350 °C, rising with filling and speed. A curve adds heating from late ignition, factory up to +70 °C at 35°. Today only knock retard goes into it. Late ignition used to reduce torque is not counted yet. The model follows the steady value with a lag, factory 1.5 s. Above the limit, factory 900 °C, protection turns on, and it turns off a hysteresis lower, factory 30 °C. While protection is active, lambda moves smoothly to the Protection lambda map (factory 0.82) if that is richer, and wideband correction is off. The model runs for each bank. Temperature map speed and filling ↓ Late ignition addition ↓ Lag model delay ↓ Limit and hysteresis protection on ↓ Protection lambda smoothly richer T steady = T map (n, rl ) + ΔT ign ; λ target = min(λ, λ prot ) when T > T limit Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 Fuel & mixture → Exhaust protection → Errors & limits Open the module Choose Exhaust protection Tempera"},{"t":"Air (filling)","u":"modules/air.html","x":"From torque to throttle Torque becomes filling, then intake pressure, flow through the throttle and its angle: through physics rather than a lever-to-percent table. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections Cylinder filling Tabs: Maps Described Throttle model Tabs: Area Described Fill calibration Tabs: Auto-calibration Described"},{"t":"Cylinder filling","u":"modules/air/fill.html","x":"Purpose This section describes how much air the cylinder takes per cycle. Fuel, torque and spark timing are all calculated from this mass. How it works The unit does not use a volumetric efficiency table. At constant speed and cam positions the air in the cylinder rises with intake pressure almost along a straight line, only shifted: part of the volume is taken by residual gas. So two numbers are enough for each speed and intake cam position. The slope says how many milligrams of air each kPa of intake pressure adds. The residual gas is the pressure at which no fresh air enters the cylinder any more. Two pressure points set the whole line, from idle to boost. The exhaust cam correction multiplies the slope, 1.0 means no effect. Charge heating covers the air being heated by the walls: the heating share by speed moves the charge temperature from the air temperature towards the coolant temperature. Factory values: slope about 7.9 mg/kPa at 500 rpm and about 9.1 at 4500, residual gas from 6 to 10 kPa by intake cam, heating from 0.25 at 500 rpm to 0.11 at 7500. For comparison, an ideal cylinder of a 6.2 L V8 at 20 °C takes about 9.2 mg/kPa. Filling in percent is relative to a cylinder f"},{"t":"Throttle model","u":"modules/air/throttle.html","x":"Purpose This section sets the flow area the throttle opens at each position. The unit uses it to find the throttle angle for the air it needs. How it works The torque asked by the lever and the idle controller becomes filling, filling becomes air mass, and air mass becomes the required intake pressure through the Cylinder filling model. Air flow equals the mass per cycle times the number of cylinders and the speed. The orifice flow equation gives the area needed for that flow with atmospheric pressure before the throttle and the required pressure after it. The area-by-position curve turns the area into an angle. If the required pressure is at least 97 % of atmospheric, the throttle opens fully. The angle comes only from the model; there is no angle trim from the intake pressure sensor yet. So an inaccurate curve changes intake pressure and torque, but not the mixture: fuel is calculated from the measured pressure. At idle the speed controller removes the error. The model has a second job: if the intake pressure sensor fails, the unit calculates the pressure from throttle, speed and barometer. While the sensor is healthy and running is steady, the unit learns an area correction from"},{"t":"Fill calibration","u":"modules/air/autocal.html","x":"Planned. This function is described in advance; part of it is not implemented yet. Purpose Fill auto-calibration is still in development; the tab says In development. This page describes how to measure the filling maps by hand and what the app will do. How it works There is no air flow sensor, so air is calculated from fuel and the wideband sensor: air equals fuel times 14.7 times the measured lambda. The filling accuracy is therefore the accuracy of the injectors and the wideband sensor. At one speed and one cam position, change the intake pressure with the throttle, and with a turbo with boost: at least three points from 40 kPa to full. At each point wait until lambda and speed settle, about 2-3 s, and record the pressure and the air corrected to 20 °C. A line through the points gives the slope and the residual gas: the residual gas is the pressure where the line crosses zero. Example at 3000 rpm: 40 kPa gives 278 mg, 70 kPa 543 mg, 100 kPa 794 mg. That is a slope of 8.6 mg/kPa and residual gas of 7.8 kPa. If the points stray from the line by more than 2-3 %, check injector small pulses, wideband warm-up, knock and exhaust protection. Planned: the app collects the points itself o"},{"t":"Torque & limits","u":"modules/torque.html","x":"Torque structure The lever and idle controller request torque, limits take the minimum, and two paths deliver it: air slowly and spark quickly. That is why limits are smooth, with no cuts. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Torque reserve More air is held than needed and late spark absorbs the excess. When torque is needed at once, spark returns to optimum within one cycle, without waiting for the manifold to fill. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Stepped protections Each cause gives only a torque fraction from 0 to 1, and the strictest applies. The lowest step is a get-home mode, not zero. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Speed governor Experimental mode, off by default: the lever sets rpm and the unit finds the torque, with propeller feed-forward. Notation: How to read a diagram Icons Abbreviations Click to enlarge. Diagram labels are"},{"t":"Torque model","u":"modules/torque/model.html","x":"Purpose This section describes the torque the engine makes at a given speed, filling, spark angle and mixture. All control rests on this model: the lever asks for torque, and the unit uses the model to find air and spark. How it works Indicated torque equals the optimal torque from the map times the efficiency of the spark lag from optimal, times the mixture efficiency and the share of working cylinders. Spark earlier than optimal also counts as a loss. Shaft torque is lower than indicated by the losses: friction from a map by speed and oil temperature, and pumping loss proportional to intake vacuum. The model works both ways. The air path finds the filling at which the base spark gives the required torque. The spark path finds the angle at which the current filling gives the required torque, but no earlier than base and no later than the latest. The angle maps are in the Ignition module. Factory values: about 6.8 Nm per 1 % filling (about 610 Nm at 90 %), spark efficiency 0.97 at 10° lag, 0.89 at 20°, 0.57 at 40°. Mixture efficiency 1.0 at lambda 0.85-0.9, 0.985 at lambda 1.0, 0.95 at 1.1. Friction 20 Nm plus 0.006 Nm per rpm, up to 30 Nm more on cold oil. Pumping loss about 0.49 "},{"t":"Coordination","u":"modules/torque/coord.html","x":"Purpose This section sets the torque the lever asks for and how fast the engine reaches it. How it works A map turns lever position and speed into shaft torque. The losses from the torque model are added to give the indicated target. While idle control works, its regulator replaces the lever request, and a slightly moved lever cannot give less than idle. Then protection limits and the speed limiter take the minimum. Limits never cut below the torque idle needs, so that the engine does not stall. Every torque rise starts softly: the first 30 Nm go at 150 Nm/s to take up the propeller drive backlash, then 2000 Nm/s. Torque falls at 3000 Nm/s. The speed limiter takes torque at once, without this smoothing. An air lead of 0.7 means: the throttle moves ahead by 70 % of the gap to the target, while spark holds the smooth rise. When the rise reaches the target, the air is already there. The factory lever map: about 540 Nm at full lever at 4200 rpm; towards a small lever the request falls faster than a straight line. Lever lever × speed map ↓ Idle replaces the request when active ↓ Limits protections and speed, minimum ↓ Smooth rise soft zone, then fast ↓ Air and spark air with lead, spark"},{"t":"Speed governor","u":"modules/torque/gov.html","x":"Purpose Experimental mode, off in the factory setup. This section turns on a mode in which the lever sets speed rather than torque. The unit picks the torque itself, so the same lever gives the same propeller thrust in any wind and at any boat speed. How it works A four-point curve turns the lever into a speed target. Factory points: 2 % - 1200, 30 % - 2500, 70 % - 4000, 100 % - 5500 rpm. The target changes no faster than 1500 rpm per second and is never below the idle target. Torque is the sum of a propeller-law feed-forward and a PI on the speed error. The feed-forward equals the learned propeller coefficient times the square of the target. Until the coefficient is learned, only the PI works. Factory gains: P 0.15 Nm per rpm, I 0.25 Nm per rpm per second. The request stays between −30 and 600 Nm. The mode switches on and off while running without a torque jump. Lever released: normal idle control works. All limiters and the smooth torque rise act as usual. Lever lever → speed curve ↓ Speed target no faster than set ↓ Propeller feed-forward coefficient × speed² ↓ PI on speed error ↓ Torque request then the usual chain M = k ff · k prop · n target 2 + K p · e + K i · ∫e dt Setup 1 "},{"t":"Limits","u":"modules/torque/limits.html","x":"Purpose This section sets the speed limit and what to do on an overspeed above it. How it works The limiter looks not at the current speed but at a forecast: speed plus its rate of rise times a lead of 0.25 s. This gives the throttle and the manifold time during fast acceleration. In a 500 rpm band below the limit the shaft torque smoothly goes to zero; at the limit the engine only covers its own losses. Torque is taken first by spark, then by air; fuel and spark are not switched off. The factory limit is 6500 rpm. The lowest of the limits acts: this one, go-home mode on a level R2 fault, and the cam-run limit. The emergency cut is off in the factory setup. If enabled, all cylinders are cut on an overspeed of 800 rpm above the limit until the speed drops below the limit. This is for the case when the propeller comes off the shaft. Speed and rise forecast with lead ↓ Band shaft torque to zero ↓ Limit lowest of those in force ↓ Emergency cut off in the factory setup Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Torque & limits → Limits → Errors & limits Open the module Choose Limits Limit Band Cut, off Press Save to ECU Set the limit From the engine and propeller data. The unit works up to 15000 rpm. Set the ba"},{"t":"Limiters: now","u":"modules/torque/limNow.html","x":"Purpose This section shows live what limits the engine now and why. How it works Data updates twice a second. The Torque block: what the engine can give now, the torque limit, the allowed share and who limits. The reason can be a fault, knock, the reaction level of a fault, the base limit map or nobody. The Fill and engine speed block: the fill limit and its share, the speed limit and who set it, reaction level R0-R3. Below are the factors of the five step protections now: 100 % means the protection is not working. At the bottom is the go-home engine speed. Only a person changes it; the factory value is 2500 rpm. Reasons faults, protections, knock ↓ Strictest share of torque and filling ↓ Limits now torque, filling, speed ↓ Who limits name of the reason Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Torque & limits → Limiters: now → Now (simulator values) Open the module Choose Limiters: now Capability Reason Speed limit R0-R3 Protections now Open the section while running Torque & limits module, Limiters: now section. Look at the reason If the engine pulls weakly, Who limits the torque names the reason. Deal with the reason Faults in the codes, knock in the Ignition module, protections in the Step protectio"},{"t":"Protections (steps)","u":"modules/torque/protect.html","x":"Purpose This section sets up protection against overheating and low oil pressure. It does not stop the engine; it lowers torque in steps so that the boat reaches the shore. How it works There are five protections: coolant, oil, exhaust (the hottest exhaust sensor, or the exhaust temperature model if there are no sensors), gearbox and oil pressure. Each has four steps: a threshold and a torque share. Factory torque steps are 100, 75, 50 and 35 %. Factory thresholds: coolant 108, 114, 120, 126 °C; oil 130, 140, 148, 155 °C; exhaust 900, 950, 1000, 1050 °C; gearbox 110, 120, 130, 140 °C. Oil pressure is compared with the minimum allowed at this speed: 100 kPa at 800 rpm, 200 at 2000, 300 at 4000, 350 at 6000. Steps of 100, 80, 60 and 40 % of it give torque of 100, 70, 45 and 30 %. Oil pressure is not checked for the first 8 s after the start. The value must stay beyond the threshold for the confirmation time: coolant 3 s, oil and gearbox 5 s, exhaust 2 s, oil pressure 1 s. Then torque falls at 0.2 per second, 0.5 for oil pressure. When the cause goes away, torque returns slowly, 0.02 per second. If the sensor is faulty, the protection does not work: the sensor fault has its own code. "},{"t":"Limiters: limits","u":"modules/torque/limMain.html","x":"Purpose This section sets the common torque and filling limits and how knock reduces filling. How it works Every limit goes through one handler: each reason gives only a share from 0 to 1, and the strictest active one acts. The torque share multiplies what the engine can give now: model torque at this speed at maximum filling with base spark, but not above the Max torque map. If no reason limits, the map itself stays as the limit. The filling share multiplies the Max fill curve, and the filling target cannot be higher. The throttle, and boost with a turbo, follow this target. The factory torque map by speed and oil temperature and the filling curve are off: 5000 Nm and 400 %. Knock reduces filling even with a healthy sensor: average retard up to 2° gives no limit, 10° and more gives 70 %. Reasons shares 0…1 ↓ Strictest minimum ↓ Capability model torque, not above the map ↓ Limits torque and filling ↓ Paths spark, throttle, boost M lim = min(M model (n, rl max ), M map ) · k tq ; rl lim = rl max · k fill Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Torque & limits → Limiters: limits → Limits Open the module Choose Limiters: limits Torque map Filling curve Filling by retard Press Save to ECU Decide on the limi"},{"t":"Limiters: air and boost","u":"modules/torque/limAir.html","x":"Purpose This section sets how much torque and filling remain on air path faults: throttle, its sensor, lever, intake pressure sensor and wastegate. How it works While the fault is active, torque and filling are at most this share of the limits. If several reasons are active, the strictest acts. Level R1 turns on only the factors, R2 also the go-home speed limit. A factor of 1 means the fault only stores a code. Factory shares, torque and filling: throttle 0.3 and 1 (level R2, throttle on its spring, torque is taken by spark); throttle sensor 0.3 and 0.4; lever 0.3 and 0.4; intake pressure sensor 0.5 and 0.6 (pressure is calculated from the throttle); wastegate 1 and 0.6. At level R2 the firmware also limits torque to 150 Nm (indicated), besides the speed limit. This value cannot be set in the app yet. Fault code and level R ↓ Path factors torque and filling 0…1 ↓ Strictest of all active reasons ↓ Limits torque and filling Setup 1 2 3 4 Torque & limits → Limiters: air and boost → Factors (simulator values) Open the module Choose Limiters: air and boost Torque and filling shares Press Save to ECU Open the section Torque & limits module, Limiters: air & boost section. Set the shares F"},{"t":"Limiters: fuel","u":"modules/torque/limFuel.html","x":"Purpose This section sets how much torque and filling remain on fuel path faults: the rail pressure sensor and the rail pressure itself. How it works While the fault is active, torque and filling are at most this share of the limits. If several reasons are active, the strictest acts. Level R1 turns on only the factors, R2 also the go-home speed limit. A factor of 1 means the fault only stores a code. Factory shares, torque and filling: rail sensor 0.25 and 0.35 (level R2 with a 3000 rpm limit, the high-pressure pump is off and injection runs on lift pump pressure); rail pressure not held 0.4 and 0.5. At level R2 the firmware also limits torque to 150 Nm (indicated), besides the speed limit. This value cannot be set in the app yet. Fault code and level R ↓ Path factors torque and filling 0…1 ↓ Strictest of all active reasons ↓ Limits torque and filling Setup 1 2 3 4 Torque & limits → Limiters: fuel → Factors (simulator values) Open the module Choose Limiters: fuel Torque and filling shares Press Save to ECU Open the section Torque & limits module, Limiters: fuel section. Set the shares For each fault: which share of torque and filling remains. 1 means no limit. Do not use zero The b"},{"t":"Limiters: engine","u":"modules/torque/limEngine.html","x":"Purpose This section sets how much torque and filling remain on engine sensor faults: coolant, air temperature, synchronisation, crankshaft sensor and knock sensor. How it works While the fault is active, torque and filling are at most this share of the limits. If several reasons are active, the strictest acts. Level R1 turns on only the factors, R2 also the go-home speed limit. A factor of 1 means the fault only stores a code. Factory shares: coolant, air temperature, synchronisation and crankshaft sensor 1 and 1, code and substitute value only; knock sensor 0.85 and 0.75 together with the safety spark retard. Fault code and level R ↓ Path factors torque and filling 0…1 ↓ Strictest of all active reasons ↓ Limits torque and filling Setup 1 2 3 4 Torque & limits → Limiters: engine → Factors (simulator values) Open the module Choose Limiters: engine Torque and filling shares Press Save to ECU Open the section Torque & limits module, Limiters: engine section. Set the shares For each fault: which share of torque and filling remains. 1 means no limit. Do not use zero The boat must reach the shore: leave enough torque to move against the current. Check Trigger the fault on a stopped or u"},{"t":"Limiters: protections and survival","u":"modules/torque/limSurvive.html","x":"Purpose This section sets how much torque and filling the protections and survivability faults add to the limit: oil pressure, overheating, oil sensors, battery, input chip, cam-run and engine health. How it works While the fault is active, torque and filling are at most this share of the limits. If several reasons are active, the strictest acts. Level R1 turns on only the factors, R2 also the go-home speed limit. A factor of 1 means the fault only stores a code. Step protections reduce torque themselves, in the Step protections section. These shares are what the fault of its path adds. Factory shares are all 1 except cam-run: 0.4 and 0.5, level R2. The go-home speed is here too, 2500 rpm in the factory setup. Only a person changes these values in the app. At level R2 the firmware also limits torque to 150 Nm (indicated), besides the speed limit. This value cannot be set in the app yet. Fault code and level R ↓ Path factors torque and filling 0…1 ↓ Strictest of all active reasons ↓ Limits torque and filling Setup 1 2 3 4 5 Torque & limits → Limiters: protections and survival → Factors (simulator values) Open the module Choose Limiters: protections and survival Torque and filling sh"},{"t":"Engine health","u":"modules/health.html","x":"Module sections Now Tabs: Signs Description coming Fuel use and range Tabs: Now Description coming Tolerances Tabs: Tolerances Description coming Propeller Tabs: Learning Description coming"},{"t":"Now","u":"modules/health/now.html","x":"What is in the application Engine health → Now Signs Live view in the application."},{"t":"Fuel use and range","u":"modules/health/eco.html","x":"What is in the application Engine health → Fuel use and range Now Live view in the application."},{"t":"Tolerances","u":"modules/health/tol.html","x":"What is in the application Engine health → Tolerances Tolerances 8 How far a sign may go from its normal value before the ECU marks it. A sign that stays outside for the confirmation time sets the «Engine health» code (level and limits - «Torque & limits»). Tolerance: propeller load Tolerance: cylinder roughness Tolerance: intake pressure vs model Tolerance: rail pressure Tolerance: fuel correction Tolerance: cylinders at the shortest pulse Tolerance: sync losses Health: confirmation time"},{"t":"Propeller","u":"modules/health/prop.html","x":"What is in the application Engine health → Propeller Learning 5 The ECU learns the propeller factor k (torque = k·n²) on steady running and then watches how the load differs from it. Here - when the running counts as steady and how slowly k is learned. Propeller: minimum rpm Propeller: steady rpm rate Propeller: steady time Propeller: learning time Propeller: minimum torque"},{"t":"CAN","u":"modules/can.html","x":"Module sections Buses Tabs: CAN1 / CAN2 Description coming Dash Tabs: Dash 6.8 Description coming OBD-II and NMEA 2000 Tabs: Outside Description coming"},{"t":"Buses","u":"modules/can/buses.html","x":"What is in the application CAN → Buses CAN1 / CAN2 Live view in the application."},{"t":"Dash","u":"modules/can/dash.html","x":"What is in the application CAN → Dash Dash 6.8 Live view in the application."},{"t":"OBD-II and NMEA 2000","u":"modules/can/out.html","x":"What is in the application CAN → OBD-II and NMEA 2000 Outside Live view in the application."},{"t":"Tools","u":"tools/index.html","x":"Trigger wheel Time per tooth and degree at different speeds. Fuel and pulse Air, fuel, pulse length, injector duty. Temperature sensor NTC sensor resistance and voltage."},{"t":"Trigger wheel","u":"tools/trigger.html","x":"Trigger wheel Change the wheel and speed to see how much time the unit has per tooth and which stroke cylinder 1 is on. Wheel 60-2 36-1 12-1 Engine speed Cycle angle Pause One tooth - Gap - One degree - Cycle, 720° - Rotation is slowed down. Numbers are for the selected speed."},{"t":"Fuel and pulse","u":"tools/fuel.html","x":"Fuel and injector pulse An estimate for sizing injectors and checking maps. Move the values and see what runs out first. Displacement Cylinders Engine speed Intake pressure Air Volumetric efficiency Lambda Injector at 100 bar Rail pressure Opening delay Air per cycle - Fuel per cycle - Pulse - Pulse angle - Duty - A simplified estimate for one pulse per cycle, gasoline (14.7). For tuning, take values from the application."},{"t":"Temperature sensor","u":"tools/ntc.html","x":"How it works The sensing element is an NTC thermistor: resistance falls as temperature rises. The sensor is connected to ground and forms a voltage divider with a 10 kΩ reference resistor from 5 V. Temperature the sensing element heats up ↓ Resistance falls along the NTC curve ↓ Input voltage divider with the reference resistor ↓ The unit calculates degrees using the sensor calibration Sensor characteristic Temperature Resistance at 25 °C β coefficient what is it? Sensor resistance - Input voltage - State - Example for a typical sensor. Take exact values from your sensor's datasheet. What R25 and β mean A thermistor curve is described by two numbers. R25 is the resistance at 25 °C, where the curve is anchored. β shows how quickly resistance falls as the sensor heats up: the larger β, the steeper the curve. It is measured in kelvin, typically 3000…4500 K for automotive sensors. R(T) = R25 · e β · (1/T − 1/298.15) , with T in kelvin: T = °C + 273.15 Example: three sensors with the same 2500 Ω at 25 °C but different β. At 25 °C they are identical; at operating temperature they diverge. The last column shows the error if the sensor's β is 3500 but the unit is set to another value. β Re"}];
